{"offset": 0, "next": 100, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fefac882c02c55a744db45fc1e6541f7fdd9d2f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07372", "CorpusId": 295593587}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fefac882c02c55a744db45fc1e6541f7fdd9d2f5", "title": "EnvOpt: Automated Optimization for Agent Environments", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07372, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526347592", "name": "Ming-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2345007132", "name": "Huan-Zhi Mao"}, {"authorId": "2385785771", "name": "Qiu-Yang Mang"}, {"authorId": "2384921668", "name": "Han-Chen Li"}, {"authorId": "2326242154", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}, {"authorId": "2257301126", "name": "A. Dimakis"}], "abstract": "An LLM agent's cost and success depend on its model, its harness, and its environment. In addition to the widely studied directions of parameter tuning and harness optimization, we propose the third direction: optimization over stationary environments. Agents are often deployed in rich environments shared by many tasks, such as a company's Slack workspace or a complex collection of data sources, documents or tickets. We call these environments stationary environments. We introduce EnvOpt, a method for automated end-to-end optimization of stationary environments for any given model and harness, using real or synthetic tasks without needing any graders or reference answers. On AutomationBench, EnvOpt reduces the cost of Claude Haiku 4.5 by 72.7% and raises its pass rate by 14.34%. The same environment transfers to Opus 5, cutting its cost by 68.1% with a 1.34% gain. Added on top of Meta-Harness's optimized harness for Haiku 4.5, it lowers cost by a further 83.4% and adds 11.17% accuracy over Meta-Harness alone. Across 5 benchmarks, EnvOpt significantly reduces the cost of agents while improving or maintaining pass rate, and is transferable to different models and different harnesses. We also discuss common successful and rejected environment optimization strategies during automated optimization. More broadly, our results show that how information is represented to an agent shapes both its cost and its success, making the environment worth optimizing in addition to the parameters and the harness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4f9bf5f37928b145ee87376920787f12254bd963", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07123", "CorpusId": 295594828}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4f9bf5f37928b145ee87376920787f12254bd963", "title": "PromptOps: Iterative Prompt Optimization for LLM-as-a-Judge Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07123, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526805815", "name": "Sven Kleinkop"}, {"authorId": "2236919194", "name": "Tam\u00e1s Janusko"}, {"authorId": "2219932364", "name": "R. Bochnia"}, {"authorId": "1701202832", "name": "G. Hempel"}, {"authorId": "2370933791", "name": "Steffen Tomschke"}, {"authorId": "3157164", "name": "J\u00fcrgen Anke"}, {"authorId": "2316056844", "name": "Maik Thiele"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as automated judges for evaluating the behavior of chatbots and other AI systems. In deployment, however, their agreement with the intended human evaluation policy may degrade as user interactions and score distributions change. Existing automatic prompt optimization methods are primarily studied as one-shot procedures on static development sets and therefore provide limited guidance for maintaining LLM judges over time. We frame this problem as judge drift and introduce PromptOps, a closedloop framework for monitoring and iteratively recalibrating LLM-as-a-Judge (LLMaaJ) systems. PromptOps separates four operational decisions: when to optimize, which optimization procedure to apply, which feedback records to use, and how to update the judge prompt. We instantiate the framework with five prompt optimization methods: GEPA, SIMBA, Rubric Synthesis, and exemplar selection using either simulated annealing or a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator. Our experiments demonstrate that iterative prompt optimization can recalibrate LLM judges and recover performance after controlled shifts in the referencescore distribution. However, effective maintenance is inherently contextdependent: neither optimization algorithm choice nor update timing generalizes universally across settings. These findings establish prompt optimization for deployed LLM judges as an inherently iterative maintenance problem and demonstrate that PromptOps provides a practical abstraction for studying and implementing this process."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e184f396a5a2fb1d5cbeaaa07b6faaa3fd0e3fbe", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07054", "CorpusId": 295594295}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e184f396a5a2fb1d5cbeaaa07b6faaa3fd0e3fbe", "title": "Reclassification Is All You Missed: Surfacing Variant Reinterpretation Need in Online Health Communities at Population Scale", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07054, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Biology"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2121640794", "name": "Kamalakkannan Ravi"}, {"authorId": "2368678189", "name": "Meng-Shu Nie"}, {"authorId": "2382747456", "name": "Annapurna Poduri"}, {"authorId": "2368716344", "name": "Cong Liu"}], "abstract": "Genetic variants are routinely reclassified in ClinVar after a patient is tested, sometimes from uncertain to pathogenic, yet patient recontact is inconsistent, with no clear duty and responsibility diffused among labs, providers, and patients. Many affected people discuss their results in public online health communities that clinical recontact often does not reach. We built PRISM, an agentic large-language-model pipeline that screens these posts, checks whether the variant was later reclassified, and drafts a disclaimer-bearing outreach message, in a no-contact dry run. Across 3.9 million posts from 32 communities, agent-predicted reinterpretation-relevant discussion is rare (0.288%) but concentrated in specialist genetics communities (6.30% versus 0.10% in general medical-advice communities) and growing fivefold since 2014; PRISM surfaces 123 candidate classification differences, 5 of them from an uncertain submission to pathogenic or likely pathogenic. On a held-out set of 30 real-post drafts, a clinician judged 27 factually grounded and 15 fit to send: factual accuracy is necessary but not sufficient for clinically acceptable outreach. PRISM contributes a population-scale reinterpretation-need map with code and data to be released upon publication."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05139", "CorpusId": 295508181}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "title": "Meta$^2$: Precomputing Reusable Knowledge for Online Harness Evolution in Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05139, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340603961", "name": "Lingzhi Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2526199633", "name": "Yi-Guang Cai"}, {"authorId": "1390516186", "name": "L"}, {"authorId": "2526386878", "name": "Si-Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2202174378", "name": "Fei-Jiang Han"}, {"authorId": "2509361763", "name": "Yongkyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2307917108", "name": "Udari Madhushani Sehwag"}, {"authorId": "2398004564", "name": "Fu-Rong Huang"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization improves long-horizon agents by refining the prompts, tools, memory, and hooks around a frozen model. Offline methods can learn from completed trajectories, but deploy one shared harness that may overfit training tasks and cannot react to evidence revealed during a new run. Online evolution can adapt before, during, or after one run through an evolver, but may have to reason about every intervention from scratch or rely on verifier feedback that is usually unavailable at test time. We introduce Meta$^2$, which moves offline optimization one level up: rather than optimizing a shared harness, it learns reusable knowledge for harness evolution and applies it within each task online. Offline, Meta$^2$ learns a textual Meta-Skill $M$ by contrasting training rollouts of the same task agent under three configurations: without an evolver, with an evolver guided by the current version of $M$, and with an evolver given privileged verifier information available only in training. Contrasts in trajectories and outcomes are aggregated across tasks to refine $M$ into reusable guidance on when and how to evolve, when to leave the harness unchanged, and which interventions to avoid. Online, an evolver combines the frozen $M$ with emerging trajectory evidence to decide whether to KEEP the harness unchanged or EVOLVE it through a task-local edit. Each edit applies to the rest of the same run without resetting its environment or progress. Thus, evolution guidance transfers across tasks while harness edits remain task-local, and held-out verifier feedback is never available to the evolver. On two long-horizon benchmarks, Terminal-Bench 2.1 and DeepSWE 1.1, Meta$^2$ achieves the highest performance among evaluated methods with GPT-5.6-Terra, reaching 81.00% and 44.44%, respectively. Ablations support the value of contrastive evolution guidance and keeping online harness edits task-local."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2324c3efadd71eb298f63a601c1d30af893faedb", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05454", "CorpusId": 295589141}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2324c3efadd71eb298f63a601c1d30af893faedb", "title": "Agentic Behavior Engineering: A Design-by-Contract Methodology for Specifying, Verifying, and Governing Enterprise AI Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05454, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2525953853", "name": "Yang-Xiao Song"}], "abstract": "Enterprise teams that deploy large language model (LLM) agents inherit a maintenance problem that traditional testing does not address. The model is non-deterministic, but the larger risk is that most of what changes an agent's behavior (a retired model version, a revised policy, an upstream schema change, a prompt edit meant to fix one case) originates outside the owning team and is discovered as a production defect rather than caught as a regression. This paper presents Agentic Behavior Engineering (ABE), a platform-agnostic methodology that treats observable agent behavior, rather than the prompt or the model, as the version-controlled engineering artifact. ABE adapts Design by Contract to non-deterministic systems. A specification-only behavior contract with stable rule identifiers states what the agent must do, must not do, and when it must abstain for lack of evidence; a lightweight scenario library turns each rule into concrete situations; and every engineering artifact (tests, runtime policies, telemetry) references the contract in one direction only, so that specification and implementation can change independently without drifting apart. The method organizes the work into five responsibilities (Specify, Ground, Control, Verify, Observe), treats missing evidence and a verified negative as different things, enforces authority outside the model, gates releases on per-scenario pass^k reliability rather than aggregate accuracy, and classifies every production run against the contract so that each finding routes to the asset that must change. We illustrate the method with an executable capacity-allocation boundary suite and a constructed worked example of a product-review approval workflow."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "feff399911e07e5adf525b31d9a1656088f7c6a0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02989", "CorpusId": 295502290}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/feff399911e07e5adf525b31d9a1656088f7c6a0", "title": "Finding Errors Is Not Enough: Selective Model Escalation for Entity-Targeted Sentiment Analysis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02989, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308473310", "name": "Nishan Chatterjee"}, {"authorId": "2265965933", "name": "Matthew Purver"}, {"authorId": "2294101380", "name": "Antoine Doucet"}, {"authorId": "1491181728", "name": "Senja Pollak"}], "abstract": "Improving a sentiment classifier with limited resources involves more than simply deciding how much data to collect or which model to train. A labelled collection may lack examples of an important class. Once trained, a classifier may benefit from another model even when that model is weaker on its own. We study these decisions in document-level Entity-Level Sentiment Analysis (ELSA), primarily using South Slavic news. We first compare acquisition orders as collections grow. We then use uncertainty to choose which predictions to revisit, how many to process, and whether an alternative encoder or local LLM can correct them. Finally, we select complete routing policies on development data for held-out evaluation. Minority-prioritised acquisition helps at some very small training sizes, but not consistently thereafter. On SentiCoref, an oracle correcting all errors in the least-confident 10% without harming correct predictions would add 9.14 Macro-F1 points; however the best evaluated action gains only 0.90. Encoder averaging helps on AspectBench and NewsMTSC but harms SentiCoref, where selective LLM assistance is more promising. Both development-selected policies route 30% of records to the encoder-assisted LLM, yielding observed gains of 1.02 points on SentiCoref (interval includes zero) and 1.71 on NewsMTSC, at substantially different inference costs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03178", "CorpusId": 295503623}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "title": "CoworkOS:Scaling Agent Coworkers for Real-world Automation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03178, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "4376097", "name": "Jiajie Jin"}, {"authorId": "2316562231", "name": "Guan-Ting Dong"}, {"authorId": "2499629513", "name": "Cheng-Jun Pan"}, {"authorId": "2526383219", "name": "Xin Deng"}, {"authorId": "2455322947", "name": "Zhuo-Hui Sheng"}, {"authorId": "2486909710", "name": "Guo-Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2352016379", "name": "Tao Gui"}, {"authorId": "1900406", "name": "Yutao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2526363415", "name": "Yuxuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "30000816", "name": "Shi-Hong Yi"}, {"authorId": "2365380853", "name": "Bin-Ze Hu"}, {"authorId": "2382833241", "name": "Zhi-Xiong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2495119597", "name": "Zhi-Chao Sun"}, {"authorId": "2375822021", "name": "Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2503895275", "name": "Han Han"}, {"authorId": "2526383123", "name": "Ming-Yu Ma"}, {"authorId": "2499651393", "name": "Jun-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "2526390285", "name": "Xiao-Tao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2495439365", "name": "Hui Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2302980850", "name": "Xin-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2282543731", "name": "Wanghan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2391873065", "name": "Ya-Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2513111", "name": "Zhecan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269420111", "name": "Honglin Guo"}, {"authorId": "2343324260", "name": "Xiao-Yu Xing"}, {"authorId": "2522801396", "name": "Xiao-Qiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2455424509", "name": "Yu-Lun Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2144456832", "name": "Xiaoxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2399060433", "name": "Jia-Hang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2362570366", "name": "Shao-Hua Liu"}, {"authorId": "2042683163", "name": "Shi-Han Dou"}, {"authorId": "2218237934", "name": "Zhi-Heng Xi"}, {"authorId": "2374299112", "name": "Qi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2394203475", "name": "Yu-Gang Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2241348826", "name": "See-Kiong Ng"}, {"authorId": "2468147446", "name": "Ya-Pan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2257036129", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}, {"authorId": "2186578511", "name": "Ji-Rong Wen"}, {"authorId": "2257039188", "name": "Zhi-Cheng Dou"}], "abstract": "Rapid advances in cowork agents are shifting human-AI collaboration toward real-world automation, delivering software, designs, and other artifacts. However, such productivity work demands both high-quality delivery on complex productivity tasks and continued monitoring and decision-making over long horizons, a combination that remains an open challenge. We introduce CoworkOS, a general-purpose harness for long-horizon work that separates task strategy from execution management: the model decides actions, delegation, and acceptance, while the runtime maintains state and feedback. Its three-layer architecture enables direct execution or delegation to Subagents, synchronously or asynchronously, under stable responsibilities. Through a shared application layer, humans and agents inspect and revise the same evolving documents, presentations, CAD models, and Blender scenes, so human-in-the-loop participation can enter both through conversation and through direct modification of the work itself. To sustain continuity across agents and sessions, CoworkOS further introduces hierarchical workspaces and persistent semantic memory, so working files, key decisions, and long-running Job logs remain shareable, reusable, and resumable after interruption. Across 12 benchmarks and 6 categories of real-world applications, CoworkOS proves highly competitive with frontier agent applications such as Codex and DeepSeek Harness. As a sustained-work case study, CoworkOS uses GLM-5.3 as its backbone to conduct RSI-driven automated post-training on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B. Over five rounds, the model's average score across eight benchmarks rose from 33.19 to 42.95 (+9.76). Looking ahead, we believe CoworkOS opens a meaningful path toward general cowork-agent intelligence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03618", "CorpusId": 295503160}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "title": "Strategist: Evolving Reasoning with Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03618, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308418220", "name": "Taiming Lu"}, {"authorId": "2326321062", "name": "Qi Xu"}, {"authorId": "2105837204", "name": "Zhi-Gang Hua"}, {"authorId": "2349957935", "name": "Zhuang Liu"}], "abstract": "A model is only half of an AI agent system. The other half is the inference strategy: the reasoning procedure that turns model calls into a response. Today that second half is written by hand, and the experience of building one strategy never carries to the next. We introduce Strategist, a meta-agent that evolves inference strategies across tasks. Each strategy is written as a blueprint,executable code that other strategies can reuse, and together the blueprints form a library. Given a task and a small development set, Strategist retrieves related blueprints, writes candidate strategies, tests them on real samples, and revises them from the results. Every candidate then enters the library, so later tasks build on earlier discoveries. On 30 benchmarks, Strategist beats the strongest baseline on 24 and matches its average accuracy (74.7% vs.74.0%) at 39% lower inference cost. Its strategies also transfer to unseen tasks and other models without further search. The part of an AI system that is not improved on its own now does."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9805caff04c195a5540b7550e40855304226e4f2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04032", "CorpusId": 295506993}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9805caff04c195a5540b7550e40855304226e4f2", "title": "ROCm InferaSim: Cut LLM Serving Trial Costs and Deploy the Best Configuration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04032, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510644315", "name": "Anshu Raina"}, {"authorId": "2281075036", "name": "Yao Fu"}, {"authorId": "2274733587", "name": "Jie-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2510643349", "name": "Peyman Razaghi"}, {"authorId": "2526253916", "name": "Shiksha Patel"}, {"authorId": "2526389862", "name": "Ravi Gupta"}, {"authorId": "2373643913", "name": "Deval Shah"}, {"authorId": "2173872376", "name": "Adeem Jassani"}, {"authorId": "153560261", "name": "Subhajit Dutta Chowdhury"}, {"authorId": "2526103935", "name": "Lorri Rao"}, {"authorId": "150172144", "name": "Kailash Gogineni"}, {"authorId": "2370975408", "name": "Zhen-Yu Gu"}, {"authorId": "2526102819", "name": "Onil Gunawardana"}, {"authorId": "2380526598", "name": "Emad Barsoum"}], "abstract": "Serving an LLM efficiently depends on the engine, how the model is split across GPUs, precision, scheduler settings, speculative decoding, KV-cache management, and prefill-decode disaggregation. The best configuration depends on the model, hardware, workload, and objective. Operators usually find it by trial and error. Exhaustively measuring the candidates in our study would take about 25,000 GPU-hours. ROCm InferaSim predicts serving performance from a few short measurements. Choices such as batch size, prompt length, and GPU count change the amount of work per GPU without changing the kernels. ROCm InferaSim benchmarks each distinct set of kernels once, on at most one node. We call this measurement an anchor. Analytical laws carry that anchor to other deployments that run the same kernels. For multi-node deployments, ROCm InferaSim reduces the configuration to a single-node equivalent, measures it, and scales the result analytically. A simulator replays multi-turn traffic, and a tuning agent searches configurations using the predictions. On AMD Instinct MI355X GPUs with vLLM, SGLang, and ATOM, 11 anchors took 9.1 GPU-hours, against 204 GPU-hours to measure the 226 fixed-length configurations they cover. On 172 configurations, time per output token has 8.1% median error. On a multi-turn coding workload, throughput is within 8%. For 159 disaggregated deployments, none used as an anchor, throughput is within 5.5%. A five-candidate short list contains a deployment within 12% of the best feasible pairing of model and latency target."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "563e7283598c54f4ade87b809f84eb885f8e18ed", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.52825/ocp.v11i.3664", "CorpusId": 295544971}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/563e7283598c54f4ade87b809f84eb885f8e18ed", "title": "ELLMO at LLMs4OL 2026 Tasks Flagship, Reuse, and Taxonomy: Ontology Learning Across Contexts", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://www.tib-op.org/ojs/index.php/ocp/article/download/3664/3936", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.52825/ocp.v11i.3664?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.52825/ocp.v11i.3664, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2383614774", "name": "Ryn Gray"}, {"authorId": "2335667582", "name": "Portia Cooper"}, {"authorId": "2455491692", "name": "Ena Nayak"}, {"authorId": "2383600654", "name": "Ryan Roche"}, {"authorId": "146253930", "name": "Brendan Donohoe"}, {"authorId": "2222418", "name": "J. Murdock"}, {"authorId": "2247302521", "name": "Douglas C. Crowder"}], "abstract": "Ontology learning aims to transform unstructured or semi-structured information into structured representations of domain knowledge. Large language models (LLMs) offer promising capabilities for automating parts of this process, but their effectiveness depends on the type of information available, including source text, partial ontologies, and related ontologies from other domains. This paper presents our submission to the LLMs4OL Challenge 2026, where we address three ontology-learning tasks: the Flagship Task, focused on ontology construction from text; the Reuse Task, focused on extending a partial ontology using text and existing triples; and the Taxonomy Task, focused on identifying taxonomic relations in a target domain using ontologies from other domains. Across these tasks, we develop task-specific methods that combine LLM prompting, NLP-based extraction, ontology context, cross-domain classification, and embedding-based downselection. Our work explores how LLM-assisted ontology-learning workflows can be adapted to different forms of textual and structured input."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "15a4bed7a41bda01ab0d33952dcb73e17769d5ab", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.00752", "CorpusId": 295502992}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/15a4bed7a41bda01ab0d33952dcb73e17769d5ab", "title": "Reading Between the Chapters: A Graph-Free RAG Rebuilt With AI and Human Feedback", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00752, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2398906012", "name": "Dipto Sumit"}, {"authorId": "2526023847", "name": "Syeda Fabiayee Jahan"}, {"authorId": "3448847", "name": "Farig Sadeque"}], "abstract": "Many questions about a book can only be answered by connecting passages chapters apart, such as a cough heard three times across a horse's life. We introduce CrossChapterQA, 1,853 such questions over 14 public-domain books. Each question lists the two to six chapters it needs (spanning half the book at the median) and the relation linking them, cites evidence from each, and has two independent reference answers graded blind. We use it to build LadderRAG, which indexes a book as passages, summaries and book-level cards for about $0.41 and answers a question for under a cent. Its first version failed mostly at retrieval. We redesigned it four times, steered by an LLM judge's verdicts and our analyses of failed answers: a design-level counterpart of RLAIF and RLHF in which feedback changes the architecture, not model weights. We also report the two redesigns that made answers worse. On the 191 questions its first version got wrong, the final version retrieves over twice as many required chapters on held-out books (recall 0.36 to 0.79), and ten annotators rate its answers 6.5/10 on average (764 ratings)."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "73abbf7ebfcddc9eaa9d9efeecee1e71ba1d5f2b", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.55708/js0510001", "CorpusId": 295425658}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/73abbf7ebfcddc9eaa9d9efeecee1e71ba1d5f2b", "title": "PRISM: Probabilistic Reasoning through Intelligent Situational Modeling - A Dynamic Context Engineering Framework for Uncertainty-Aware Agentic AI in Healthcare Administrative Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://www.jenrs.com/?sdm_process_download=1&download_id=51184", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBYSA", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.55708/js0510001?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.55708/js0510001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2333655054", "name": "Muthukumarapandian Chandrasekaran"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) used in healthcare administrative workflows often fail because the context they receive is incomplete, not because the model lacks capability. In prior authorization, a retrieved document set can be topically relevant yet still miss the evidence that a payer policy requires. Physicians report completing about 45 prior authorizations per week and 94% report associated care delays, so reliable automation matters. This paper presents PRISM (Probabilistic Reasoning through Intelligent Situational Modeling), a Dynamic Context Engineering (DCE) framework that runs as pre-inference middleware between healthcare data systems and an LLM. PRISM composes six mechanisms: Context Evidence Sufficiency scoring (CES), a Clinical Criteria Chain (CCC), Criteria-Seeded Gap retrieval (CSG), Context Mutation Tracking (CMT), Adversarial Context Hardening (ACH), and Uncertainty-Governed Escalation (UGE). Together they check a context bundle against policy requirements, retrieve missing evidence, record every transformation, and escalate when context quality is insufficient. On 1,200 synthetic prior authorization cases authored against InterQual 2024 criteria, PRISM reaches a determination F1 of 0.912 against 0.769 for dense retrieval with chain-of-thought prompting (18.6% relative improvement) and 0.710 for BM25 retrieval (28.5%), and lowers context brittleness from 0.158 to 0.049 (69.0%). Because the cases and the PRISM policy templates share one source, these results are an upper-bound estimate that requires confirmation on independently annotated and real-world data."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "31fcb9ec2bee6868189d28d205680d78c2a06bfd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38392", "CorpusId": 295373262}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/31fcb9ec2bee6868189d28d205680d78c2a06bfd", "title": "Masked Erosion: How Acceptance Gates Lose Redundant Safeguards in Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38392, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2424570721", "name": "Hau-Sing So"}, {"authorId": "2187282034", "name": "Dai-Xian Liu"}, {"authorId": "2342512497", "name": "Wei-Cai Long"}], "abstract": "Self-improving LLM agents edit their own harness, the prompts and rules around a fixed model, and keep an edit only if an acceptance gate scores it higher on a batch of tasks. Such a gate sees what the harness does under the current model, not what it would still prevent if one safeguard failed. When safeguards overlap, deleting one is masked: the others still block the harm, so the gate decides on task success alone and redundancy erodes unseen. We measure harm for every subset of four written safeguards in two agent environments and run 411 improvement loops under ten gate rules, mostly as controlled deletion experiments, plus three runs of the GEPA optimizer. A deletion behind another working safeguard adds almost no observable harm. A paired significance test blocks deletions that pass by chance but not deletions that genuinely raise task success, and a larger evaluation batch lets it delete cheaper safeguards: at the same cost, 20-task batches kept every harness protected while 40-task batches lost all six. Large harm audits prevent exposure but not the loss of depth, which turns into harm when the harness is reused with a weaker model. GEPA kept every safety rule verbatim, yet two of its three final harnesses misused credentials under a weaker model. A statistical model frozen before the runs predicts where each gate's protection breaks as safeguard cost and batch size change, and freezing safety text or auditing one-layer ablations preserves depth."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f71ac9f17d18289f30066e643458154e03e53c50", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38120", "CorpusId": 295372794}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f71ac9f17d18289f30066e643458154e03e53c50", "title": "Post-Training Large Language Models with LLMON", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38120, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "1720805839", "name": "Basel Shbita"}, {"authorId": "2326296307", "name": "Michael Hind"}, {"authorId": "123819783", "name": "Chad DeLuca"}, {"authorId": "2287921064", "name": "N. Fulton"}, {"authorId": "15132482", "name": "Shubhi Asthana"}, {"authorId": "2354133833", "name": "Farhan Ahmed"}, {"authorId": "32779570", "name": "Anisa Halimi"}, {"authorId": "24018646", "name": "Ya-Da Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2282987505", "name": "Mauro Martino"}, {"authorId": "2356001264", "name": "David D. Cox"}, {"authorId": "1891570", "name": "D. Gutfreund"}], "abstract": "Large Language Models (LLMs) treat all context tokens uniformly, lacking explicit separation between executable instructions and passive data. This leads to prompt injection vulnerabilities, ambiguous instruction binding, and unreliable execution control. We present a post-training methodology using LLMON (LLM Object Notation), a framework that enables separating instructions from data and requires explicit identifier-based execution. We construct a large-scale structured training corpus of 4.6B tokens spanning general, RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), and tool-calling data, augmented with distractor-infused variants in which competing instructions and irrelevant data segments are present in context but not referenced. Post-training two Granite-4.1 base models (3B and 8B) through supervised fine-tuning followed by reinforcement learning, we show that LLMON-structured supervision reduces attack success rates on indirect prompt-injection benchmarks from 53-100% to 0.02-5.6% while preserving general capabilities. We further introduce attention-based interpretability metrics that diagnose failure modes at individual transformer layers for structured inputs, revealing how our approach reshapes internal attention patterns. Our results demonstrate that explicit execution semantics can be taught through standard post-training without architectural modification."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a95b44d59e0037a1c26a4b1d72b5aac07fc3a4a0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38960", "CorpusId": 295373363}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a95b44d59e0037a1c26a4b1d72b5aac07fc3a4a0", "title": "Efficient Agentic Audio-Visual Question Answering through Toolset Pruning and Modality Consolidation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38960, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2154462254", "name": "Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2118915448", "name": "Tian-Zi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371136807", "name": "Peter Bell"}], "abstract": "Fine-grained audio-visual question answering (AVQA) requires models to resolve complex cross-modal correspondences and temporally grounding from sparsely distributed evidence. Omni-modal agents, which autonomously orchestrate perception tools to actively collect evidence, achieve strong performance, but it remains unclear whether active perception from both audio and visual modalities depends on broad specialized toolsets, especially audio or visual modality-specific tools. Such systems may also introduce long execution trajectories and substantial inference cost. We investigate whether comparable performance can be retained with a compact workflow exposing only essential perceptual capabilities. Starting from the specialized-tool OmniAgent, we conduct staged controlled experiments that prune the toolset and progressively consolidate modality-specific tools into omni-modal tools, motivating a two-tool agent comprising only Omni-modal captioning and Omni-modal QA. Across three widely used AVQA benchmarks, our compact agent achieves comparable or better accuracy than the specialized-tool agent while reducing inference cost by around 70%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "242ce440a52d34a1396f2084bf0597a0e2cc0fc7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36774", "CorpusId": 294621174}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/242ce440a52d34a1396f2084bf0597a0e2cc0fc7", "title": "Batching the Reflective Optimization Loop: Parallel Proposals Make GEPA Faster and Better", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36774, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510652391", "name": "Jia-Lin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2347536533", "name": "Lakshya A. Agrawal"}, {"authorId": "2275830490", "name": "Shang-Yin Tan"}, {"authorId": "2263095684", "name": "Donghyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2311119790", "name": "Lutfi Eren Erdogan"}, {"authorId": "2347540207", "name": "Dan Klein"}, {"authorId": "2347540442", "name": "Koushik Sen"}, {"authorId": "2297674853", "name": "Alexandros G. Dimakis"}, {"authorId": "2288590948", "name": "Matei Zaharia"}], "abstract": "Reflective text optimization makes agentic systems better, but the optimization loop is itself a resource-hungry workload whose sequential structure leaves parallel evaluation capacity idle. We extend the open-source GEPA optimizer to propose and evaluate a batch of candidates on each optimization step instead of one candidate at a time. In our sweep on two tasks, most batched runs finished in half the wall-clock time or less, and the fastest in about a quarter to a third. Batched settings also achieved higher held-out test scores, from 68.9% to 72.1% on LiveBench-Math (with $2\\times2$) and from 49.0% to 60.0% on HoVer (with $8\\times1$). A simple runtime model explains the measured speedups under a finite worker pool, and batched settings also spend their metric-call and dollar budgets more efficiently. We frame the axes along which a reflective optimization step can scale in parallel, a first look at allocating a fixed optimization budget across them."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4279baac4b3395341960afa51a9ad05c71ee5b31", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35077", "CorpusId": 294366250}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4279baac4b3395341960afa51a9ad05c71ee5b31", "title": "Improving VLMs on Scientific Chart Analysis via Autonomous Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35077, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340228333", "name": "A. Dubey"}, {"authorId": "2520783531", "name": "Vidushi Goel"}, {"authorId": "2520897857", "name": "Kiran Rana"}, {"authorId": "2520470570", "name": "Shikhar Mathur"}, {"authorId": "8769315", "name": "Vaggos Chatziafratis"}], "abstract": "Understanding and operating with scientific charts is a standard approach in data analysis, and while humans are good at interpreting such charts, open vision-language models (VLMs) are known to perform poorly when it comes to question answering based on such data types. Motivated by an initial observation that many of their errors are due to inexact answering, we raise the following natural question: how much accuracy can we recover without changing the model at all? In this paper, we study this question by searching over inference programs and the code around a\"frozen\"version of the model. Using ideas from autonomous evolution (famously used in AlphaEvolve), we first start from a baseline inference program, and in each round we show an LLM judge the code together with a few failures on training questions, and ask for an improving evolutionary move. As usual in autonomous evolution approaches, the candidate programs are kept in a small pool ranked by training accuracy, and the next edit is performed on top-scorers from this pool. We run this approach on three VLMs and four popular benchmarks on scientific charts, and we measure the fraction of descriptive questions whose answer exactly matches the ground truth. The accuracy improves in all experimental settings. On average, it rises from 16.6% with the starting program to 41.3% with the evolved one, and the largest single increase is 55.2 percentage points. As a side benefit, our evolved program is 3.8x faster than the starting program, because short answers are easier to grade and faster to generate. Our results demonstrate that a large part of the observed errors arise due to the inference pipeline, and that optimizing it can improve the\"frozen\"VLM without the need to retrain it."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "02bfc41201571241bed2f14d859e7c09ba7eddc6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34722", "CorpusId": 294366187}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/02bfc41201571241bed2f14d859e7c09ba7eddc6", "title": "CEPO: A General Framework for Improving Prompt Optimization in Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34722, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2182393860", "name": "Jongha Kim"}, {"authorId": "2284333010", "name": "Seohyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2519998887", "name": "Jonathan Minho Chung"}, {"authorId": "2393040212", "name": "Jinsung Yoon"}, {"authorId": "2108926667", "name": "Hyunwoo J. Kim"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent systems (MAS) require both effective coordination and specialized task execution. Existing prompt optimization approaches typically couple these functions within agent-specific prompts. We introduce Coordinator-Executor Prompt Optimization (CEPO), a general framework for improving prompt optimization in MAS. CEPO separates system-level coordination from agent-level execution through a shared coordinator prompt and agent-specific executor prompts, and guides their updates according to their respective roles. This structure allows shared coordination rules to be refined consistently across agents while keeping execution instructions separately adjustable. CEPO provides the prompt structure and optimization guidance, while existing prompt optimizers perform the underlying search. Extensive experiments spanning 11 benchmarks across four domains demonstrate that applying CEPO to four existing prompt optimizers consistently improves their performance. Ablations and qualitative analyses support the proposed design, while controlled studies on a document-understanding MAS further demonstrate improved optimization budget efficiency and performance scaling with the number of agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5d1e7d0dfddec480f49bbd4bba906041c625109f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34027", "CorpusId": 294366607}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5d1e7d0dfddec480f49bbd4bba906041c625109f", "title": "When Does GEPA Merge Help? A Mechanism-Level Diagnostic", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34027, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2524370572", "name": "Chong Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2520400185", "name": "Ru-Yi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2518248038", "name": "Chang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2519873336", "name": "Alice Zhao"}], "abstract": "GEPA is among the strongest reflective prompt optimizers for language-model programs, but its system-aware merge operator is unstable across models: merge improves gpt-4.1-mini on average while degrading Qwen3-8B, an asymmetry noted but left unexplained by the GEPA paper. We investigate this instability across five datasets and two models. Our nine-configuration sweep over merge timing and merge operator shows that merge can improve both models when configured appropriately. This suggests that merge instability is better explained by configuration sensitivity than by an inherent flaw in the merge operator. We then test whether prompt-level features predict GEPA's merge-accept decisions and find that they do not, ruling out static-feature gating. As a proof of concept, a behavior-based probe built from these diagnostics avoids large merge failures in our multi-seed runs by abstaining on high-risk tasks, although it does not consistently outperform NoMerge. Code is available as GEPA-MergeProbe at https://github.com/ChongZZZZ/GEPA_MERGE ."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "157d8ae99621745e6cf3a8126db565f3980e9f15", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33450", "CorpusId": 294227990}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/157d8ae99621745e6cf3a8126db565f3980e9f15", "title": "Secure and Functional Code Generation with Self-Evaluation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2482123569", "name": "Md Afif Al Mamun"}, {"authorId": "2325093115", "name": "Gias Uddin"}], "abstract": "Large Language Models (LLMs) can generate functionally correct code that remains insecure. Security-focused fine-tuning can be expensive and is not always available, motivating inference-time approaches. Security-oriented prompting and retrieval can reduce vulnerabilities, but may also remove required behavior or apply defenses incompatible with the task. The challenge is to identify relevant security knowledge and use it to improve security without sacrificing functionality. We present SecGent, an inference-time framework that retrieves weakness context from the Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) database and applies it to secure code generation. By connecting task specifications to relevant textual CWE records, SecGent provides task-specific security guidance without relying on retrieved vulnerable or patched code examples. Complementary functional and security tests check the resulting implementation, and their execution feedback guides repairs toward satisfying both objectives. We evaluate SecGent with four open-source LLMs and Claude Sonnet 5.5 across five programming languages on the CWEval benchmark. It achieves the highest joint functional--security success against four inference-time baselines for all five models. Across the open-weight models, mean functional-security@1 (FS@1) improves by 15.5% over RAG, with gains in both functionality and security, and by 19.8% over the strongest prompt optimizer compiled for each model. On Sonnet, FS@1 reaches 92.1%, outperforming the strongest baseline AutoGen by 4.8%. In a separate comparison, SecGent outperforms Claude Code with security-focused prompting by over 6% in FS@1. Feedback-guided repair further improves FS@1 by up to 6.7% over generation without feedback, primarily by recovering functionality while checking security."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "58d69ce3befdaa474805aee075656ce0f68c06c2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33240", "CorpusId": 294227857}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/58d69ce3befdaa474805aee075656ce0f68c06c2", "title": "NewsBench: Expert-Grounded Evaluation of Epistemic Quality in AI News Reporting", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33240, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2519072245", "name": "Matt Wilde"}, {"authorId": "33772445", "name": "Jillian R. Fisher"}, {"authorId": "2328150395", "name": "Andrew B. Hall"}, {"authorId": "2520178619", "name": "Kathryn Salam"}, {"authorId": "32137583", "name": "Robert S. Goldfarb"}], "abstract": "As frontier AI systems increasingly serve as sources of political and current-events information, evaluating the epistemic quality of their responses is now especially consequential. Existing evaluations often focus narrowly on ideological alignment, rely on measures not validated for this setting, or depend on reference labels derived from public opinion or non-expert annotation, potentially producing misleading or miscalibrated results. To address these limitations, we introduce NewsBench, an expert-grounded benchmark that reframes evaluation around editorial standards developed by senior practitioners in journalism, policy, and intelligence analysis. NewsBench operationalizes quality across three dimensions, source quality, factuality, and neutrality, using a two-phase pipeline in which domain experts first define and calibrate evaluation guidelines, which are then transferred to automated AI judges validated against expert-adjudicated gold labels. The benchmark includes approximately $3,000$ evaluation prompts, with roughly $500$ refreshed monthly to capture emerging current events, roughly $2,500$ human expert-annotated items for calibration and validation, and a suite of AI judges designed to enable scalable evaluation. To validate our methodology, we report both inter-rater reliability among human experts and AI judge--human agreement across all three dimensions. Finally, we apply NewsBench to four frontier models, revealing distinct and non-overlapping failure profiles in sourcing, factuality, and neutrality."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2b6a8796c41c2be528454dce0e564e2b7c4acbb0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31436", "CorpusId": 293894418}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2b6a8796c41c2be528454dce0e564e2b7c4acbb0", "title": "Conservative Meta-Agent Search: Architecture Adaptation under Non-Stationarity", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31436, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2385463345", "name": "A. Alzahrani"}], "abstract": "Automated agentic-system design is typically evaluated on static task suites with immediate scalar rewards. Deployed systems face persistent shift, delayed dependent feedback, correlated component failures, and costly architecture changes. We formalize meta-agent architecture search as sequential incumbent replacement with switching costs and hard risk constraints and introduce Conservative Meta-Agent Search (CMAS). CMAS retrieves archived architectures and proposes typed graph edits, then scores each against the incumbent by paired shadow replay under common random numbers, with interventional component credit. Promotion requires an empirically calibrated, variance-penalized simultaneous score above a pre-specified margin, and is followed by a simulated canary stage and pre-specified rollback. Since backtests cannot certify meta-level skill, we introduce MetaInvest-Bench, a regime-switching simulator whose specified worker competence makes a surrogate-optimal architecture computable, with leakage-controlled replay, adversarial governance tracks, and five negative controls. Financial streams serve as a risk-sensitive testbed for meta-level generalization; we claim no alpha. Across 96 streams, CMAS cuts normalized mean surrogate dynamic-oracle regret from 0.116 to 0.104 and raises held-out utility from 0.44 to 0.48 over the strongest adaptive baseline; when extra workers add no value, it contracts to near-single-agent teams. Its deployment inference cost is ~6% lower, though its search-inclusive token cost is the highest of the methods compared. The observed per-update false-promotion rate is 3.4% against a nominal 5% target, and 4.8% in a sealed rerun. The calibration is empirical: it concerns an update-level replay statistic on an author-designed benchmark, and guarantees neither full-utility improvement, nor absence of simulated canary-stage harm, nor sequence-level error control."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31709", "CorpusId": 293936006}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "title": "Mining the Design Space of Multi-Agent Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31709, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "83579257", "name": "Xavier F. Cadet"}, {"authorId": "2313693827", "name": "Jun-Yan Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2327213146", "name": "Peter Chin"}, {"authorId": "46666605", "name": "Kyle Richardson"}], "abstract": "Designing large language model (LLM) multi-agent systems (MAS) still relies on trial and error and informal\"folk wisdom.\"Manual studies test only a few communication structures or topologies (e.g., sequential, parallel, shared-memory, or debate), while automated optimizers discard search trajectories after selecting a task-specific workflow. We present a two-phase pipeline for mining reproducible design principles from workflow code and execution traces. We repurpose AFlow's MCTS optimizer across six composable operators and 26 deterministically verified Enigmata tasks, retaining all intermediate rounds to build a corpus of 28,568 executable workflows. A sandboxed AutoResearch loop then mines orchestration code and prompt templates under an $L_0$-penalized validation objective and tests candidate principles on held-out searches. Across 300 feature-mining trials, we identify effects that persist on held-out searches across three levels of workflow design: workflows beginning with independent solvers and one-shot aggregation (Star-first) perform worse ($\\Delta = -0.045$); persistent shared documents (SharedMemory) outperform debate ($\\Delta = +0.135$); and prompt specificity and placeholder-based context routing yield gains comparable to communication-structure selection ($\\Delta = +0.118$). More broadly, our pipeline and results show the feasibility of turning discarded search trajectories into a statistical testbed for discovering and validating multi-agent workflow design principles."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6d685aaeb4e60ad53f14ffd014bdfbb360e50a15", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27417", "CorpusId": 293808871}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6d685aaeb4e60ad53f14ffd014bdfbb360e50a15", "title": "KPHarness: Co-Evolving Knowledge and Probing for Long-Horizon Gaming Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27417, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2344949577", "name": "Zhi-Kang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2408588322", "name": "Yue-Qing Sun"}, {"authorId": "2392914911", "name": "Sen Cui"}, {"authorId": "2336613191", "name": "Ziang Ye"}, {"authorId": "2409933062", "name": "Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2412097369", "name": "Yan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2209255184", "name": "Xie-Rui Song"}, {"authorId": "2391755644", "name": "Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2397877875", "name": "Ting-Ting Zhu"}], "abstract": "A failed gaming trajectory reveals that an agent needs intervention, but not necessarily how its execution harness should change. Effective harness adaptation therefore requires gathering diagnostic evidence, rather than merely proposing alternative recovery rules. We present KPHarness, a framework that adapts the external execution harness of a frozen vision-language-action model through knowledge-probing co-evolution. Recovery knowledge supplies context-specific intervention hypotheses that guide active probing; probe outcomes reveal when these hypotheses apply and provide evidence for refining both recovery knowledge and later probe selection. This reciprocal process allows the harness to improve not only which interventions it applies, but also how it investigates failures. KPHarness separates fast policy execution from a slow research loop that translates accumulated knowledge and probing evidence into bounded harness revisions. Each revision is compared with the incumbent under matched sandbox conditions and accepted only when the paired evaluation shows improvement. Recoveries supported by further validation are retained for cross-task reuse, while immediate interaction feedback and delayed episode-success credit update action- and knowledge-probing policies during adaptation. Experiments on Minecraft tasks spanning mining, combat, and crafting show improved task coverage over bare policy execution, a manually specified fixed harness, and knowledge-only harness evolution with non-adaptive probing. Evaluation on previously unseen tasks further shows improved task completion with the evolved harness, knowledge bank, and probing policies held fixed,"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27435", "CorpusId": 293809157}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "title": "Harness Self-Evolution for Long-Horizon Terminal Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27435, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328617416", "name": "Ru-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2249001715", "name": "Yu-Cheng Shi"}, {"authorId": "2375157272", "name": "Zong-Xia Li"}, {"authorId": "2403718571", "name": "Jun-Yao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2268636229", "name": "Zhong-Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2447743497", "name": "Yue Yu"}, {"authorId": "2238955130", "name": "Hai-Tao Mi"}, {"authorId": "2292161506", "name": "Dong-Ruo Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2422670781", "name": "Leoweiliang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon terminal agents may solve a task in one run yet fail across repeated runs. Stochastic errors can propagate through actions and context, creating a gap between capability and reliable execution. We present RiseHarness, a framework for iteratively improving agent harnesses with model weights fixed. It focuses on flipping tasks with both successful and failed runs under the same configuration, using successful runs as references for failure analysis. RiseHarness combines agent trajectories with internal harness traces from a runtime monitor to examine how model actions interact with command execution, feedback, context management, and completion decisions. A Harness Handbook links this evidence to harness behaviors and source code, helping identify recurring problems in mechanisms shared across tasks. RiseHarness then searches other harness frameworks for relevant implementations and adapts them as design references to the current model and execution protocol. Candidate changes undergo behavioral checks and repeated task evaluation; versions with measured gains seed the next iteration. This loop integrates suitable mechanisms from different frameworks into a single harness and uses new execution evidence to guide further changes. We evaluate task success and reliability across models, ablate harness execution evidence and external research, and test transfer to unseen benchmarks with the same model. RiseHarness treats a specific model as the unit of harness adaptation, studying how changes to shared interaction mechanisms can make existing capabilities more reliable across tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a3a5f1fca75a18597391f10aa4857a260744a448", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27816", "CorpusId": 293811632}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a3a5f1fca75a18597391f10aa4857a260744a448", "title": "RAG-NIMBF: A Non-Invasive, Modular Benchmarking Framework for Automated RAG Configuration Analysis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27816, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2516047048", "name": "N. Hart"}, {"authorId": "2327244936", "name": "Nicolas Weeger"}], "abstract": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has become the dominant paradigm for grounding the outputs of large language models (LLM) in external knowledge. However, practitioners are faced with a vast configuration space comprising chunking, embedding, retrieval, reranking, prompting and generation models, and there is limited guidance on how these components interact. We present RAG-NIMBF, a modular benchmarking framework that attaches to existing RAG pipelines as a non-invasive evaluation layer. It evaluates the configuration space through controlled ablation with end-to-end experiment tracking. The evaluation combines LLM-based answer quality scores, retrieval correctness measures, and runtime indicators. This study validates the framework across multiple generation models on the SQuAD dataset. This process reveals non-obvious interactions and demonstrates how the experiments are tracked. The findings indicate that the retrieval balance of relevance and diversity substantially enhances the relevance and coverage of retrieved context in comparison to a conventional similarity search, with MMR raising nDCG@5 by +4 to +11 percentage points across the swept models. More detailed prompt instructions meaningfully increase how faithfully generated answers stick to the retrieved evidence, especially for capable and reasoning models (+10 to +13pp on Qwen3.6-27B, GPT-4o-mini and Qwen3.5-think, but negligible on the 20B generator). Overall, RAG-NIMBF transforms the configuration space of RAG into reproducible, comparable experiments. By attaching to existing production pipelines in a non-invasive way, it enables practitioners to systematically evaluate and improve deployed systems without disrupting existing workflows. This replaces ad hoc choices with evidence-based decisions."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28914", "CorpusId": 293844720}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "title": "D-Skill: Distilling Teacher Knowledge through Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28914, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379614165", "name": "Shuo Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2265517766", "name": "Da-Cheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2346365389", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents excel at complex tasks, but high inference costs make transferring their capabilities to smaller models essential. Traditional knowledge distillation achieves this through parameter updates, requiring access to the student's weights and additional training compute while encoding the transferred knowledge implicitly in the parameters. We introduce D-Skill, a framework that treats the teacher as a black box and distills its behavioral advantage into an interpretable natural-language skill by analyzing paired rollouts and identifying behavioral divergences and agreements. The resulting lessons are consolidated offline, with any compatible skill-update backend, into a skill document that is provided to the student at inference time. D-Skill constructs the skill entirely offline from fixed trajectories, without labels, further environment interaction, or weight updates after rollout collection. Across SpreadsheetBench-Verified, ALFWorld, and chess puzzles spanning multiple teacher-student pairs, D-Skill closes 75%-95% of the teacher-student gap without touching model weights. After a single offline pass over the paired traces, D-Skill outperforms one-epoch SFT baselines and remains competitive with multi-epoch SFT. Under the same model family, the resulting student also incurs 5.7x-8.3x lower inference cost per task than the teacher. Our results demonstrate that natural-language skill distillation offers a performant, interpretable, and efficient alternative to weight-based capability transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c5051d1506146949eac6d934910aa1d4b97fd59c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28815", "CorpusId": 293844719}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c5051d1506146949eac6d934910aa1d4b97fd59c", "title": "Forging More Tasks into Better Harnesses: Agentic Harness Evolution via Successive Scale Racing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28815, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2282543720", "name": "Jing-Zhe Liu"}, {"authorId": "1736632", "name": "Ji-Liang Tang"}, {"authorId": "2362540948", "name": "Tong Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2310871156", "name": "Neil Shah"}, {"authorId": "2336871459", "name": "Clark Mingxuan Ju"}], "abstract": "Agentic harness evolution optimizes an agent's instructions, tools, and control flow by using its task execution experiences as evolving evidence. While more evolution tasks should intuitively improve performance, our preliminary study reveals a paradoxical failure mode: the performance of existing methods degrades as the task pool expands. Our further analysis demonstrates that this degradation stems from suboptimal evidence selection during optimization, as different evidence combinations lead to substantially different evolution outcomes. To address this issue, we propose Successive Scale Racing (SSR), a framework that adaptively searches for optimal evidence combinations in each evolution round. SSR constructs diverse evidence combinations based on the corresponding task quality and coverage, and efficiently races the resulting candidate harnesses under a small compute budget. Empirically, across three distinct benchmarks, SSR achieves state-of-the-art results in all six configurations. Notably, as the task pool grows from 40 to 200, SSR steadily improves the pass rate from 64.3% to 68.3%, while the baselines'performances all drop. Furthermore, its racing mechanism identifies near-optimal candidates while reducing validation executions by 60%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "24486996b4335287e9b60f2bee9794911c3d2e35", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26020", "CorpusId": 293802981}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/24486996b4335287e9b60f2bee9794911c3d2e35", "title": "Understanding When Reflective Prompt and Skill Optimization Helps on Locally Served Reasoning Models: A Two-Model Study of GEPA and SkillOpt", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26020, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515957910", "name": "Andrea Giummarra"}, {"authorId": "2515952939", "name": "Carmelo Baglieri"}, {"authorId": "2288416935", "name": "Antonio Di Maria"}, {"authorId": "2302774517", "name": "A. Farruggio"}, {"authorId": "2515958098", "name": "Michele Piatto"}, {"authorId": "49779401", "name": "G. Micale"}], "abstract": "Reflective text-space optimizers such as GEPA and SkillOpt have reported large gains on frontier and mid-size models. We test replication across two reasoning-native target-serving configurations: DeepSeek V4 Flash through DwarfStar and Qwen3.8-27B through LM Studio. For each target, we evaluate the same held-out tasks and four conditions - minimal baseline, GEPA prompt, SkillOpt skill, and manually engineered prompt - on HotpotQA (n=500 per condition) and MATH-500 (n=250). In the primary Qwen v2 rerun under the same frozen protocol, GEPA selects the HotpotQA seed and exactly reproduces baseline behavior, whereas SkillOpt's validation gain reverses into held-out degradation. On MATH-500, GEPA raises exact match from 0.828 to 0.904 while reducing latency but increasing token consumption, so lower response time does not imply lower token cost. DeepSeek likewise shows its strongest GEPA effect on MATH-500 (0.852 to 0.908). Across both targets, GEPA's strongest gains occur on MATH-500, while neither optimizer improves the HotpotQA baseline. Optimization is therefore task- and method-dependent, and validation gains may reverse on held-out evaluation. An earlier Qwen run is retained as serving-sensitivity evidence. These results suggest that textual headroom for a model-task pair and the observed effect of a text-space optimizer are more informative when decomposed into content/reasoning, answer-realization/evaluator alignment, and serving-completion/reliability. The cross-benchmark pattern further suggests that optimization may benefit from low-ambiguity, actionable evaluation signals."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "463d7424dc2cd1ac6bd0977452ace23d1f26cec1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25568", "CorpusId": 293805984}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/463d7424dc2cd1ac6bd0977452ace23d1f26cec1", "title": "Design and Cross-Domain Evaluation of an Agent Execution Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25568, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515943883", "name": "Saurav Joshi"}, {"authorId": "2512763805", "name": "Manas Gupta"}, {"authorId": "2480250845", "name": "A. Raman"}, {"authorId": "51170347", "name": "Akshay Budhkar"}], "abstract": "Completing complex tasks with language-model agents requires coordinating planning, execution, and review so the final result addresses the user's original objective. We present an agent execution harness that keeps the original objective separate from a revisable plan and an execution record. A planner assigns scoped tasks, workers produce outputs and artifacts, and a reviewer examines results against the original request before submitting or requesting additional work. Reusable domain-specific instructions guide these roles and are refined during construction using task inputs and execution trajectories, without official grading feedback. We evaluate complete harness configurations across terminal tasks, clinical responses, application building, and scientific questions. On Terminal-Bench 2.1, the harness passes 83 of 89 instances (93.26%); on HealthBench Professional, a Fable-configured system achieves a length-adjusted rubric score of 69.63 across 525 tasks. In a 53-task paired HealthBench comparison, the Fable-configured harness achieves an aggregate score 19.89 points higher than direct completion, despite a $-1.46$-point median task-level change; mean inference cost rises from \\$0.29 to \\$2.13 per task. We combine these outcomes with latency, documented instruction revisions, and reviewer decisions. Evaluating outcomes alongside execution trajectories and resource use clarifies how coordination supports task completion, where it introduces regressions, and at what cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aa543084d38335554ed7e38db80ef9c40ae390c3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24545", "CorpusId": 293686891}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa543084d38335554ed7e38db80ef9c40ae390c3", "title": "When Code Becomes an LLM Call: An Empirical Study of Call-Site Contracts in Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24545, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2238663708", "name": "Shi-Hao Weng"}, {"authorId": "2268821983", "name": "Long-Tian Wang"}, {"authorId": "2407676790", "name": "Yang Feng"}, {"authorId": "2265746458", "name": "Ming-Fei Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2127092888", "name": "Jia Liu"}, {"authorId": "2365452274", "name": "Chao Shen"}, {"authorId": "2409959854", "name": "He Ye"}, {"authorId": "2260686724", "name": "Federica Sarro"}, {"authorId": "2318972909", "name": "Xiao-Fei Xie"}], "abstract": "Software quality has long rested on specifications that state what code must do, and on testing and verification that check that the code does it. Agentic systems change this foundation: they replace parts of their logic, such as choosing the next tool, judging whether an action is allowed, or writing code to run, with calls to a large language model (LLM). The program still relies on what this logic must guarantee, but the model's behavior is not fixed by code, varies across runs, and can be steered by its input. The guarantee must therefore come from the program around each LLM call site, as a contract of pre-conditions, prompt constraints, and post-conditions. We present an empirical study of these contracts in 496 LLM call sites of 40 popular open-source agentic systems (13.4 MLOC, four languages). Using a multi-agent pipeline validated by 17,428 human judgments, we trace where every model output flows, derive an expected contract for each call site without access to its code, and compare it with the contract that the code implements. We find that 48% of LLM outputs reach control flow or execution, and 73% of these never reach a person. Programs enforce 78% of their constraints in code, but in the prompt, 67% of the constraints are only requested from the model. Although 82% of call sites are checked before and after the call, 63% of the expected constraints are unmet and only 5.3% of call sites satisfy their whole contract; ten recurring patterns explain 99% of the gaps. 38 mechanisms behind these gaps trigger at runtime, and a small code fix that enforces the expected constraint stops all 38. Our results show that the presence of checks says little about their adequacy, and we derive implications for agent developers, framework maintainers, and researchers."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "48d65c87a630337dc8c0376e1c6bc063b331d1ec", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24861", "CorpusId": 293804518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48d65c87a630337dc8c0376e1c6bc063b331d1ec", "title": "FlowMaker: From Tasks to Cost-Efficient Generative AI Workflow Deployments", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24861, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2341713401", "name": "Dimitrios Liakopoulos"}, {"authorId": "2279751412", "name": "Prasoon Sinha"}, {"authorId": "2397000", "name": "N. Yadwadkar"}], "abstract": "Generative AI workflows execute user tasks by composing sequences of LLM calls, where each call performs a sub-task (e.g., summarization or translation), forming a workflow. Executing such workflows requires decisions over workflow structure, model assignments, and deployment configurations, which jointly determine latency, cost, and accuracy under dynamic workloads and resource constraints. Existing systems optimize these decisions in isolation or fix them early, leading to suboptimal cost and goodput. We make two key observations. First, efficient serving requires jointly exploring all combinations of workflows, model choices, and deployment configurations under dynamic cluster state. Second, reuse of deployed LLMs is critical for reducing cost, but must be performed on-the-fly, as reuse depends on current load and resource availability. Together, these make selecting a workflow-variant-a combination of workflow, models, and deployment configuration-a time-critical problem. We present FlowMaker, which exposes a task-level interface and selects workflow-variants on behalf of users. To realize this, we design a constraint-guided load-aware pruning algorithm for fast, on-the-fly decisions. FlowMaker improves goodput by up to 10.14$\\times$ and reduces cost by up to 2.77$\\times$ compared to state-of-the-art baselines, including Murakkab."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23574", "CorpusId": 293546054}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "title": "Darwin-Claw: Environment-Adaptive Experience Consolidation for Recursively Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23574, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297186228", "name": "Ming-Ze Yin"}, {"authorId": "2380043925", "name": "Xiao-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316958318", "name": "Dian Li"}, {"authorId": "2515057402", "name": "Long-Fei Luo"}, {"authorId": "2362798356", "name": "Jia-Hong Yan"}, {"authorId": "2166681156", "name": "Yiheng Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2307736278", "name": "Chang-Yu Hsieh"}], "abstract": "While Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across complex tasks, they predominantly operate in isolated sessions, lacking the ability to continually learn from historical interactions or adapt to user-specific environments. Existing self-evolution paradigms often indiscriminately inject raw interaction trajectories into memory or skill modules, leading to obscured behavioral patterns and compromised update controllability. Furthermore, as foundational LLM reasoning improves, the bottleneck for agentic evolution shifts from accumulating generic knowledge to effectively managing situational experience. To address this, we propose Darwin-Claw, an environment-adaptive self-evolving framework that systematically distills episodic experiences to drive the comprehensive evolution of operational skills and durable memory. Theoretically grounded in an iterative agent-actor and evolver-critic co-adaptation, Darwin-Claw introduces a Hierarchical Experience Consolidation System that extracts fine-grained error attributions and generalizes them into dynamically updated prototype vectors. This structured situational experience serves as interaction patches for context-sensitive perception and explicit optimization signals for skill refinement. Practically, Darwin-Claw is a readily deployable, automated framework compatible with mainstream agent systems without requiring manual curation. Extensive experiments on WildClawBench demonstrate that Darwin-Claw yields substantial performance improvements, establishing a controllable and stable pathway for the continual, user-centric adaptation of autonomous agents in real-world ecosystems."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c127cd6f739f71893e1286e91ae193bffa1a8ca1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24248", "DOI": "10.18653/2026.emnlp-main.2446", "CorpusId": 293685960}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c127cd6f739f71893e1286e91ae193bffa1a8ca1", "title": "Probing Ambiguity: Adversarial Intent Resolution for Robust Text-to-SQL", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.emnlp-main.2446.pdf", "status": "BRONZE", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24248, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379723791", "name": "You-Bing Yin"}, {"authorId": "2309117796", "name": "Xu-Jun Peng"}, {"authorId": "2374452103", "name": "Muhammad Shahin Uddin"}, {"authorId": "2379663935", "name": "Erin Babinsky"}, {"authorId": "2335576396", "name": "Da-Ben Liu"}, {"authorId": "2335189759", "name": "Anoop Kumar"}], "abstract": "Text-to-SQL systems typically assume a one-to-one mapping between natural-language queries and correct SQL translations. In practice, however, many queries are inherently ambiguous, admitting multiple valid interpretations that yield distinct outputs. We introduce Adversarial Intent Resolution (AIR), a multi-agent framework that actively probes and resolves such ambiguity without any task-specific training. AIR first constructs competing interpretations, verifies through SQL execution that they produce genuinely different results, and generates targeted clarification questions grounded in the observed data differences. Evaluating on leading benchmarks for ambiguous questions, AIR demonstrates strong performance, efficiency, and robust cross-benchmark transferability. On the AMBROSIA benchmark under schema-only context, a realistic production setting where exposing the full database is infeasible, AIR outperforms IntentRL, the state-of-the-art reinforcement learning model, across all metrics (e.g., F1 0.679 vs. 0.618). Crucially, AIR exhibits only ~7% performance degradation without the full database, compared to ~20% for IntentRL. On the AmbiQT benchmark, where ambiguity arises from schema design rather than linguistic structure, AIR achieves higher F1 under both context settings without any benchmark-specific adaptation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b10fc309dc2f3292d91e5d66e62ed2dce45d30b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30167", "CorpusId": 293894413}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b10fc309dc2f3292d91e5d66e62ed2dce45d30b0", "title": "IRAH: Hierarchical Agentic Tree Search for Retrieval Pipeline Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30167, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2309324848", "name": "M. Cardei"}, {"authorId": "2516989750", "name": "Aniket Singh"}, {"authorId": "2502166781", "name": "A. Moghadam"}, {"authorId": "2516893659", "name": "Srividhya Baikadi"}, {"authorId": "2487623787", "name": "Rashmi Shetty"}], "abstract": "Optimizing Information Retrieval (IR) pipelines for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) requires jointly selecting structural components and numerical hyperparameters under limited evaluation budgets. We introduce the Information Retrieval Agentic Harness (IRAH), which combines Language Agent Tree Search for IR (LATS-IR) with offline meta-learned initialization. LATS-IR performs UCT-guided hierarchical search, first exploring macro-structural pipeline choices and then refining numerical parameters, with natural-language reflections to guide subsequent mutations and provide inspectable search traces. We evaluate IRAH on three in-distribution benchmarks and one out of-distribution benchmark to assess generalization under distribution shift. Under the same meta-learned initialization, LATS-IR outperforms TPE on two of three in-distribution benchmarks, with gains of up to 6.4%, and remains competitive on the out-of-distribution benchmark. Across all four benchmarks, warm-started IRAH achieves the highest average held-out nDCG@10 (0.4816) and improves over its cold-start counterpart. These results establish IRAH as a competitive approach to retrieval pipeline optimization under constrained evaluation budgets."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3088b25b3c0489f29cd67a55e16bc7869bb035c5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21218", "CorpusId": 292982299}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3088b25b3c0489f29cd67a55e16bc7869bb035c5", "title": "AutoOnboarder: Autonomously Onboarding LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21218, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2125483441", "name": "Tanay Arora"}, {"authorId": "2487088168", "name": "Cong-Lin Liu"}], "abstract": "Agentic large language models have been documented to lose reasoning capability when placed inside a harness, the software that mediates their observations and actions. To reconcile these losses, we introduce AutoOnboarder, an autonomous system that adapts an agent to a task the way a trainer onboards a new employee. It places the agent under the supervision of an intelligent ``Onboarder''coding agent, which iteratively tailors its foundation model, prompt, and harness source code to simultaneously improve performance and decrease cost. Like a real onboarding process, the Onboarder is only given tens of examples, and all evaluation data is isolated on a separate virtual machine to prevent leakage. We report the lessons learned from building and testing AutoOnboarder on genomic-sequencing prior authorization form submission. Autonomously onboarded agents are able to match or exceed the performance of a Gemini 3.5 Flash baseline at up to 29$\\times$ lower costs. However, these agents remain imperfect, and the Onboarding process varies greatly in terms of final performance. We conclude that future Onboarding systems require a robust environment for the Onboarder, including a structured thought interface, (controlled) internet access, and a wide model search space."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b438ddbe85c529f47b48632dc17402d15d8fb9a1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21040", "CorpusId": 292982039}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b438ddbe85c529f47b48632dc17402d15d8fb9a1", "title": "Recursive Reward Improvement via Adversarial Meta-Prompt Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21040, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2352019521", "name": "Wei Dai"}, {"authorId": "2479098149", "name": "Qian-Chu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2394074209", "name": "Guang-Hui Qin"}, {"authorId": "2365514724", "name": "Xing Han"}, {"authorId": "2264107059", "name": "Tristan Naumann"}, {"authorId": "2376536810", "name": "Paul Pu Liang"}, {"authorId": "2337095081", "name": "Sheng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) has gained wide attention for its ability to work with limited data. In a proposer-solver loop, a proposer model writes training tasks and a solver model learns to solve them through reinforcement learning. Such loops have succeeded in mathematics and coding, where every answer can be checked automatically. Open-ended expert tasks, like clinical report generations, have no answer key, which makes simple question-answer generation not viable. Here the proposer must also write a rubric that lists the content an answer should include and the errors it should avoid. However, the unique challenge behind model-written rubric is the comprehensiveness of it. For naively generated rubric, solvers tend to exploit it, naming the expected topics without the substance behind them, which corrupts the learning process. To solve this problem, we introduce RRI, Recursive Reward Improvement, which uses a reward hacking adversary to supervise the reward. Before the solver trains on a generated task, an adversary writes an answer that games its rubric while being as useless as possible, the same model writes an honest answer without seeing the rubric, and up to two repair steps revise the rubric to make the honest answer win. The exploits found also rewrite how future tasks and rubrics are generated. On HealthBench Professional, RRI improves the adjusted score over the Generation Only self-improvement baseline by 10.9 points at 9B and 12.4 at 27B, and by 10.3 points without any external model. In particular, the gains are largest on difficult and writing tasks. We show that the 27B model trained on the HealthBench Professional description improves by 6.6 points on PRBench Hard and 8.0 on ProfBench without seeing a task from either. We further analyze the exploitable points of model-generated question-rubric pairs to facilitate future research on open-ended self-play."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5f5e317ae5465cb4a23ef07673362cc3f7750b8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21796", "CorpusId": 293004597}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5f5e317ae5465cb4a23ef07673362cc3f7750b8", "title": "Reflective Compositional Grounding for Natural-Language-to-SPARQL", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21796, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2264258732", "name": "Isabelle Mohr"}, {"authorId": "2403984765", "name": "John Dujany"}, {"authorId": "2316638121", "name": "Andr\u00e9 Freitas"}], "abstract": "Natural-language query generation over large knowledge graphs embodies the classical vocabulary problem in human-systems communication. Questions express concepts through wording and compositions that can differ substantially from the target schema, while limited model context requires selective access to schema information. We present an NL2SPARQL framework that places conceptual compositionality at the center of query interpretation and connects it to the relational composition required for binding and query construction. A fixed concept encoder represents compound expressions jointly, and schema candidates are ranked using semantic similarity, type compatibility, and hierarchy proximity. Reflective refinement critically examines how the grounded meanings are realized through argument bindings, joins, and operator scope. Diagnosed semantic discrepancies guide revisions to the responsible stage. We formalise a monotone refinement discipline that accumulates scoped interpretation requirements and establish conditions under which changes to the composed process preserve previously validated interpretations. Across five benchmark/knowledge-graph targets, mean schema recall@10 increases from 0.712 to 0.837. With retrieval held fixed, reflective prompt rewriting adds 2.27-3.33 answer-F1 points. These experiments evaluate the grounding and refinement mechanisms, while the formal analysis specifies the stronger requirements for conservative adaptation across queries."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22466", "CorpusId": 293453108}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "title": "Harness-Delta Attribution: Dissecting Gains From Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22466, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2357365368", "name": "Wen-Xuan Ding"}, {"authorId": "2365376107", "name": "Qin Lu"}, {"authorId": "2302894470", "name": "Chang-Long Yu"}, {"authorId": "2405778950", "name": "Sha Li"}, {"authorId": "2244738638", "name": "Shuo-Wei Jin"}, {"authorId": "2362222390", "name": "Xin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2408193613", "name": "Zhen-Yu Shi"}, {"authorId": "2271320260", "name": "Greg Durrett"}], "abstract": "Harness evolution automatically optimizes the prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration code around a fixed-weight model, offering a way to improve agentic systems without updating model weights. The agent harness is iteratively optimized to achieve higher performance on the search set. But how much of this gain reflects benchmark-specific overfitting or additional inference-time compute, and how much may generalize beyond the search set? In this work, we introduce Harness-Delta Attribution (HDA), an evaluation protocol that dissects an evolution gain into artifact-driven overfitting, gains explained by test-time scaling, and the remainder, which gives an upper bound on residual generalizable improvement. We apply HDA to 11 executor models and three harness-evolution frameworks across math, coding, creativity, and agentic tasks. In 21 of these settings that show statistically significant search-set gains, we find that a large share of the improvement is explained by overfitting or test-time scaling. Specifically, among these 21 settings, overfitting and test-time scaling account for 54% and 14% of the gain on average; their combined share exceeds 50% in 14 settings. The residual and held-out transfer vary substantially across tasks, models, and frameworks. Importantly, search-set gains often fail to transfer to held-out tasks, and provide limited evidence that they will persist under practical distribution shifts. Moreover, validation-based signals are insufficient to prevent overfitting. These results motivate attribution-aware evaluation in future harness evolution research."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "116305fdf5f011a8c30b8b0cc98407be923dafe9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19323", "DOI": "10.18653/2026.findings-emnlp.2248", "CorpusId": 292903215}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/116305fdf5f011a8c30b8b0cc98407be923dafe9", "title": "TopicMerge: A Divide and Conquer Approach to Topic Modeling", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.2248.pdf", "status": "BRONZE", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19323, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2221128059", "name": "Matthew Raffel"}, {"authorId": "2272959539", "name": "Li-Zhong Chen"}, {"authorId": "2258957941", "name": "Sai Munikoti"}, {"authorId": "24029613", "name": "Sameera Horawalavithana"}], "abstract": "Recent advancements in topic modeling have split into two main paths: efficient embedding-based methods that offer scalability but are often outperformed on standard clustering metrics, and Large Language Model (LLM) approaches that achieve state-of-the-art clustering performance but at the cost of prohibitive computational expense and poor scalability. This paper introduces TopicMerge, a novel multi-stage pipeline that bridges these approaches to achieve both state-of-the-art clustering performance and computational efficiency. TopicMerge first employs an efficient clustering pipeline to deliberately over-segment a document corpus into a large set of fine-grained, semantically pure micro-clusters, which are then given initial semantic labels. It then introduces a novel, iterative merging algorithm where an LLM, guided by local neighborhood context, arbitrates on consolidating candidate pairs. These approved merges are then selectively executed and semantically relabeled by the LLM, hierarchically building broader, more coherent topics. Extensive experiments on the Bills and Wiki datasets show that TopicMerge achieves state-of-the-art clustering performance while reducing required LLM calls by over 92% compared to document-level methods, offering a practical and scalable solution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19424", "CorpusId": 292902687}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "title": "VideoHarness-SRSI: Structured Recursive Self-Improvement for Video Understanding Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2254334449", "name": "Zi-Hao Lin"}, {"authorId": "2309801263", "name": "Hao-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2379955487", "name": "Hao-Bo Xu"}, {"authorId": "2339005997", "name": "Chi-Chih Chang"}, {"authorId": "2266396687", "name": "Xu-Ying Ning"}, {"authorId": "2258547648", "name": "Ying-Long Xia"}, {"authorId": "2504318197", "name": "Shuang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2507464273", "name": "Xin-Yi Song"}, {"authorId": "1995922397", "name": "Dongfang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2293354444", "name": "Li-Fu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2393886238", "name": "Yang Song"}, {"authorId": "2266712798", "name": "Qi-Fan Wang"}], "abstract": "LLM system capabilities depend not only on model parameters but also on the executable harnesses that organize information acquisition, tool use, reasoning, and memory. Recursive harness evolution can automate their design, but how it can reliably discover generalizable systems from limited, noisy, and repeatedly reused feedback remains unclear. We study this question in agentic video understanding, where rich multimodal evidence and coupled mechanisms make harness optimization challenging. We first introduce VHarness-BRSI, a recursive self-improvement framework that evolves video harnesses around a frozen vision-language model. Analyzing over 4,000 evolved candidates, we find it competitive with hand-engineered systems but identify three interconnected failure modes: validation overfitting, search-budget dilution, and the early-experience trap. We trace all three to a common root: the same imperfect validation evidence drives both harness revisions and the proposer's search strategy. We therefore propose VHarness-SRSI, an evidence-governed framework in which every search decision rests on evidence that is transferable, sufficient, and gathered before the search commits. It restricts the proposer to content-free feedback, informs exploration with instrumented warm-up experiments, and develops mechanisms in isolated probe-race branches before factorial composition and joint refinement. Across four video understanding benchmarks, VideoHarness-SRSI improves the equally weighted mean held-out score by 6.2% relative to VideoHarness-BRSI and reduces the mean validation-test gap by 37.9%. Reliable harness evolution thus depends on how search is organized and informed, not only on how well it optimizes validation performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cd711d4b48411021efe44db3142df0ef700ee054", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20055", "CorpusId": 292902021}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cd711d4b48411021efe44db3142df0ef700ee054", "title": "GeoCloak: An Agentic Framework for Semantics-Preserving Geolocation Privacy via Peripheral Clue Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20055, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2127727861", "name": "Yi-Xin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2321661021", "name": "Ming-Jie Li"}, {"authorId": "2257351123", "name": "Yun Shen"}, {"authorId": "2257034706", "name": "Michael Backes"}, {"authorId": "2257291195", "name": "Yang Zhang"}], "abstract": "The rapid advancement of large vision language models (LVLMs) has made geolocation inference from images highly accurate and accessible. This poses serious privacy risks: a passive adversary with black-box LVLM access can infer a user's location from a single photo with no metadata. Existing protection methods rely almost exclusively on adversarial perturbation, which introduces perceptible visual artifacts and utility degradation. We identify the visual-geolocation asymmetry: geolocation-decisive clues, such as street signs, tend to be visually peripheral but dominate location inference. Exploiting this asymmetry, we propose GeoCloak, an agentic framework for semantics-preserving geolocation privacy protection. It acts as a clue-aware planner that analyzes each image to identify geolocation-relevant clues, dispatches targeted semantic edits to directly suppress peripheral clues, and autonomously configures adversarial perturbation to further suppress residual geolocation features, all under iterative utility-aware feedback. We evaluate GeoCloak on four diverse datasets spanning 108 countries and 576 cities against five LVLMs and three inference methods. GeoCloak reduces overall city-level accuracy from 41.2% to 9.9%, approximately half of the best-performing baseline, while achieving substantially superior utility preservation both qualitatively and quantitatively. We hope this work takes a meaningful step toward practical, semantics-preserving geolocation privacy protection, giving users greater control over the location information inadvertently embedded in their shared images."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9cf4f144bb261f0e2914f14a85fd3aa63288f2f2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18069", "CorpusId": 292897457}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9cf4f144bb261f0e2914f14a85fd3aa63288f2f2", "title": "From Agents to Edges: Paired Optimization for Multi-Agent Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18069, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2280850617", "name": "Feng Lin"}, {"authorId": "2281003213", "name": "Xiao-Tian Yu"}, {"authorId": "2280332748", "name": "Yi-Fan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2509633962", "name": "A. Li"}], "abstract": "Strong individual agents do not guarantee effective collaboration. Communication must align what one agent conveys with how another interprets and uses it. This dependency makes the communication edge a natural unit of optimization. We introduce Paired Automated Communication Optimization (PACO), which jointly optimizes the instructions at both ends of each edge and their shared communication convention in systems built on black-box language models. PACO keeps model weights, agent roles, and workflow topology fixed. A state-aware discounted bandit selects edges for refinement, while execution feedback guides paired revisions evaluated through staged end-to-end execution. The optimized instructions integrate into existing agent prompts without additional model calls at deployment. On a four-agent workflow evaluated across diverse multi-agent benchmarks, PACO consistently outperforms leading prompt-optimization and communication baselines. Configurations learned on HotpotQA retain their advantage in cross-dataset transfer without target-specific optimization. These results demonstrate that optimizing how agents communicate can substantially improve collaboration within an otherwise fixed system."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ebd8da5923ce5c6606582270fce57800bdf60145", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19148", "CorpusId": 292902191}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ebd8da5923ce5c6606582270fce57800bdf60145", "title": "Compiling Expert Procedures into Verified Programs: When a Small Model Is Enough", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19148, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2378676180", "name": "Vishvesh Bhat"}, {"authorId": "2240531495", "name": "Asad Aali"}, {"authorId": "2394310501", "name": "Muhammad Ahmed Mohsin"}], "abstract": "In procedural tool-use domains, how much of the gap between base models is procedure rather than capability? We build an explicit program from a teacher's traces and run it in front of unchanged small models, including an 8B model. The construction, PLVR , takes the reasoning traces of a teacher, Kimi-K3, clusters their segments to locate recurring operations, has a frontier model author one typed primitive per operation, each run by a small frozen model and assembles the program by A* search over a mined rule library, under a reference-action objective. At inference the program takes one of two forms: for troubleshooting and tool search it produces the chain of thought the student would otherwise generate for itself, and the student acts on it; for multi-turn tool matching the student proposes each action and the program checks, repairs, or retries it before it goes out. On the official tau2-Bench Retail and Telecom test sets and on LiveMCPBench, with three Nemotron base models and the same program for each, the answer depends on the domain. On Telecom, Granite-4.2-8B as the student goes from 43.0% to 91.2% under the program, and every Nemotron student reaches 93.3-94.4%, against at most 58.2% for fine-tuning on the same traces, prompt optimisation of the student, or two token-matched baselines. With Granite-4.2-8B as the student, the program is within 2 to 3 points of the Nemotron students on Telecom and within 3 on Retail, and 3 to 13 points below them on LiveMCPBench: where the domain is procedural an 8B student is enough, and on tool search the student still matters. On Retail and LiveMCPBench the program is competitive with the best baseline. The spread between base models shrinks from about 7 points to about 2, averaged over the suites, with no weights changed."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4e12f90d3fa6fe05b0af7b7ebee9194254dcd0b7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19226", "CorpusId": 292903497}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4e12f90d3fa6fe05b0af7b7ebee9194254dcd0b7", "title": "Agentic Self-Awareness: Aligning Knowing, Acting, and Holding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19226, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2157989543", "name": "Qing-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "50376014", "name": "Hiroaki Hayashi"}, {"authorId": "2383058705", "name": "Jia-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2383150796", "name": "Chien-Sheng Wu"}], "abstract": "Frontier AI agents increasingly act in environments where seemingly solvable tasks may lack prerequisites, contain conflicting requirements, or reveal blockers only during execution. We formulate agentic self-awareness (ASA) as the alignment of task-grounded feasibility judgments (know), warranted behavior (act), and responses to reconsideration (hold), distinguishing what a task permits from what an agent can solve. We build paired feasible and blocked tasks in Terminal-Bench 2.1 and retail $\\tau^2$-bench and introduce an authorship-aware M0-M3 rubric that separates recognizing a blocker from taking the certified deferral action for the correct reason. Separate probes assess stated feasibility beliefs and responses to a single challenge. In a Terminal-Bench protocol baseline, blocker recognition reaches 84.4%, but strict self-awareness (SA) reaches only 40.6%. Harness search without model-weight updates increases strict SA from 45.3% to 93.8% on reused Terminal-Bench search tasks; selection for abstention alone, however, can reduce success on feasible tasks. Independently initialized joint-objective searches improve both blocked-task SA and feasible-task solve on their own search sets. Retail held-out gains remain limited, and Terminal-Bench blocked-task transfer is not measured. This evaluation pipeline makes the recognition-action gap measurable and sets a two-sided target for improving agent decisions under changing task evidence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ca7b4119b5f05ab86038dec87f2c44c7bf3b46e9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15513", "CorpusId": 292896179}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ca7b4119b5f05ab86038dec87f2c44c7bf3b46e9", "title": "REPRO: Reflective Evolutionary Prompt Optimizer for Automated LLM Red-Teaming", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15513, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297694830", "name": "Abrar Alotaibi"}, {"authorId": "2373740959", "name": "Moataz A. Ahmed"}], "abstract": "Safety-aligned large language models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to adversarial prompts that bypass their safety training. Existing automated red-teaming methods either require privileged access unavailable in production, rely on manually engineered per-model templates, or optimize a single objective without population-level diversity. We present REPRO (Reflective Evolutionary Prompt Optimization), a pure black-box framework that discovers adversarial prompts through evolutionary optimization driven entirely by a compressed open-weight 7B reflective model on a single consumer GPU. REPRO introduces two mechanisms absent from prior work, both isolated in a component ablation: semantic crossover, an LLM-guided multi-parent recombination operator, and Bayesian strategy selection via Thompson Sampling informed by structured failure analysis. We catalog REPRO's behavior on AdvBench, JailbreakBench, and HarmBench across 36 target models from 11 families, including GPT-5-nano, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.5-Pro, and Grok-4.1. Under our protocol REPRO reaches 100\\% attack success rate (ASR) on every evaluated target in a single turn, at a median of 24 target queries per behavior. Two properties of the protocol qualify that figure. The judge scoring candidates during optimization also produces the reported ASR, and search halts when it accepts, so ASR reports whether search succeeded within the iteration budget rather than success at a fixed budget. All baseline figures are transcribed from their original papers, which use different judges, budgets, and model versions. We therefore present this as a catalog of model coverage under one protocol rather than a head-to-head ranking, and develop both qualifications in the paper."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6ed173a321c810337646783e0457ba3d0f68f496", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.64898/2026.10.04.756532", "CorpusId": 292904355}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6ed173a321c810337646783e0457ba3d0f68f496", "title": "An Expert-Curated Benchmark for Frontier and Local LLMs Querying a Biomedical Knowledge Graph", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2026/10/11/2026.10.04.756532.full.pdf", "status": "GREEN", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.64898/2026.10.04.756532?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.04.756532, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Biology"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510666594", "name": "Johannes F. Medagbe"}, {"authorId": "2510663860", "name": "Bonface M. Kilyungi"}, {"authorId": "2327208353", "name": "Arun Isaac"}, {"authorId": "2510664161", "name": "Alexander Kabua"}, {"authorId": "2266034589", "name": "Felix Lisso"}, {"authorId": "6248576", "name": "S. Wambua"}, {"authorId": "2273915155", "name": "Robert W. Williams"}, {"authorId": "2248281415", "name": "P. Prins"}], "abstract": "We combine GeneNetwork\u2019s 1TB structured database with its metadata as an RDF graph and an associated literature corpus. We benchmark frontier and local LLMs querying it through four architectures: document, graph and agentic retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and a combination of the three. We evaluate them on 40 domain specific expert-curated questions with a new metric: the satisfaction frequency \u03c8 (psi, i.e, the share of questions answered with precision and recall of at least 70% or an f1 of at least 60%). \u03c8 ranks systems the same way precision, recall and f1 \u2014 a machine learning metric built from the harmonic mean of precision and recall \u2014 but is easier to read and assess for humans. System architecture had a large effect: with full use of Claude Opus 4.8 the plain document RAG of our earlier GNQA system had a \u03c8 of 36% against 86% for our new combined tools. Delegating most of the work from Claude Opus 4.8 to a local model halved query costs while still reaching a \u03c8 of 59%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b76555525cfc7188cbee6f2eed7803fbde6ee246", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15389", "CorpusId": 292896333}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b76555525cfc7188cbee6f2eed7803fbde6ee246", "title": "VideoEvoHarness: Self Evolving Agent Harness from Unlabeled Videos for Video Understanding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15389, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347179648", "name": "Wei-Xin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2492556852", "name": "Yi-Fan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2263510675", "name": "Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2300173227", "name": "Ming-Fei Rong"}, {"authorId": "2484481606", "name": "Zhen-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2294147935", "name": "Zhi-Sheng Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2284669445", "name": "Sheng-Sheng Qian"}, {"authorId": "2237947496", "name": "Chang-Sheng Xu"}], "abstract": "Video understanding agents rely on a harness to acquire evidence, use tools, and reason, but improving it requires substantial effort. Recent self evolution methods seek to optimize harnesses through execution feedback to reduce human intervention. However, existing methods often rely on annotated video questions and answers or focus on updating model parameters, leaving harness evolution from unlabeled videos underexplored. VideoEvoHarness jointly evolves a Questioner and a Solver harness from unlabeled videos with frozen model parameters. The Questioner constructs questions and computes reference answers directly from video memory records. Question quality assessments and Solver execution trajectories guide question generation policy revisions. The Solver supports evolving skills, strategies, hyperparameters, and tools using execution feedback. Executable strategies define workflows in code, reducing repeated interpretation of natural language instructions. Each round, candidate harnesses are compared on identical validation questions, with validated improvements retained for later use. Experiments on benchmarks and a dedicated benchmark question set, using videos disjoint from evolution videos, show higher overall accuracy than initially and transfer gains across backbones. Overall accuracy on this set also improves progressively across rounds. This supports our foundational framework for harness evolution from unlabeled videos, opening a promising direction for self evolving video agents. Code is available at https://github.com/Wei-Xin-Xu/VideoEvoHarness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c01ea9b7ecdd2e751d93f61c256c852acadd07e1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15738", "CorpusId": 292896898}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c01ea9b7ecdd2e751d93f61c256c852acadd07e1", "title": "Visible but Not Avoided: State-Dependent Re-Proposal of Rejected Candidates in Catalog-Based LLM Optimization Loops", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15738, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2450227740", "name": "Jian-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2374285396", "name": "Wen-Hui Chen"}, {"authorId": "1807914", "name": "Chi-man Vong"}], "abstract": "LLM optimization loops often show the proposer the candidates it has already tried and rejected, but leave it to the proposer to avoid them. We ask whether avoidance measured in a static probe predicts avoidance in the states an optimizer's own trajectory produces. In catalog-based loops where three open LLMs improve a GUI agent by naming one of 40 edits, a re-proposal is identified exactly by the edit's name. Clean base-state probes underpredict re-proposal in the proposers'own loops (macro-averaged loop-minus-probe rate 0.32 [0.27, 0.37]), and proposals of already evaluated harness-edit pairs consume 28-63% of valid proposer iterations. The two settings reject different edits: a probe's rejected edits are mostly ones the proposer did not choose, whereas a loop's are its own earlier choices, which remain preferred when the history is removed. Ordinary history lowers re-proposal for two proposers but raises it for the third, and the effects of failure evidence are model-specific. Stating the rejection status in one sentence lowers re-proposal in every model (-0.16 [-0.22, -0.09] relative to a neutral list of the same IDs). For catalog-based loops with exact edit identities, rejection avoidance should be measured on the rejected sets the loop produces."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9f0d90c0e3fa20e4caf2a9959a2d75e0be742ffc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15969", "CorpusId": 292895432}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f0d90c0e3fa20e4caf2a9959a2d75e0be742ffc", "title": "PopGen: Generating Semantically Diverse Populations for Zero-Shot Coordination", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15969, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2322796738", "name": "Paul Teiletche"}, {"authorId": "2290490710", "name": "Kanghyun Ryu"}, {"authorId": "2507854459", "name": "K. Park"}, {"authorId": "7794353", "name": "Dhruva Tirumala"}, {"authorId": "2271092152", "name": "Markus Wulfmeier"}, {"authorId": "144703985", "name": "Negar Mehr"}], "abstract": "In multi-agent reinforcement learning, exposing a cooperator to partners with diverse strategies during training is key to better collaboration at test time. However, existing methods typically rely on trajectory-level proxies to measure diversity, which may label two distinct trajectories of the same strategy as diverse. Instead, our key insight is to leverage LLMs'ability to describe behaviorally distinct agents in environments, similar to how humans can recognize high-level strategies. To this end, we introduce PopGen, a framework that synthesizes semantically diverse agents from scratch using LLMs. PopGen first generates a diverse set of textual descriptions of agent behaviors, then translates each behavior into a code policy that can be executed within the environment. We show that cooperators trained against PopGen populations achieve robust zero-shot cooperation with unseen partners, outperforming population-play baselines across four collaborative environments. We further show that semantic diversity scales with PopGen population size, improving the cooperator's worst-case performance, and we identify design choices that preserve population quality while maximizing diversity. Our code is available at https://github.com/labicon/PopGen."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d15e25214f89695d00e62fbcefa5f77d0eb1b7ae", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13000", "CorpusId": 292828693}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d15e25214f89695d00e62fbcefa5f77d0eb1b7ae", "title": "Hindsight Structured Advice Distillation: Steering Executor Agents with Advisors Trained on Their Failures and Task Characteristics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13000, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2456997156", "name": "O. O. Koyluoglu"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents are used for specialized work such as financial research, healthcare and law. Such agents are often capable enough for a task but still miss its details. In finance, for example, these details include which filing reports a figure or how a company discloses a metric. Retraining the agent to learn these details is expensive, and often impossible when the agent is a closed or shared system. Writing the missing know-how into the agent's prompt makes the prompt long and identical for every input, although different inputs need different guidance. We propose to learn to generate this guidance instead. A small advisor model reads each input and writes structured advice, which is appended to the prompt of a frozen executor. The advisor is trained with hindsight, following work on distillation with privileged information. On a training pool with graded answers, we analyse the executor's failures, run it under several strategies to see which one worked on each input, and let a teacher that sees the grades and the traces write the advice that would have helped. The advisor then learns, by supervised fine-tuning, to write that advice from the input alone. Because the student is a separate small model rather than the executor, it is cheap to train and adds little latency. We call this approach hindsight structured advice distillation (HSAD). On FrontierFinance, a public benchmark of expert-written financial research queries, an 8B advisor raises the qualification rate of Samaya's proprietary research agent, which already leads the benchmark's leaderboard, from 52.9% to 56.6%. This is above the agent's own high-effort configuration, at 37% lower cost per query. We study the generalization of the proposed approach, and in particular show that HSAD also improves results on WebShop, BIRD text-to-SQL and ExpertLongBench."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a98093fd2ac4ed83bd7ce778d8548fd15b8f7e8c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13287", "CorpusId": 292830623}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a98093fd2ac4ed83bd7ce778d8548fd15b8f7e8c", "title": "Do Prompt Optimizers Break Requirements They Are Not Scored On? A Preregistered Study", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13287, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2115652172", "name": "Sangdo Han"}, {"authorId": "2311303558", "name": "Moonsoo Kim"}], "abstract": "Prompt optimizers rewrite a prompt to raise one target metric, but prompts also state requirements the metric does not score, such as an output format, citations, or abstention. In a preregistered study, we run GEPA, MIPROv2, and TextGrad on HotpotQA, SQuAD 2.0, and IFEval and count per-item violations of unscored requirements with deterministic checks. By the preregistered criterion, all three optimizers produce regressions, substantially on HotpotQA, where net regression in citation validity reaches 10.7 to 41.6 points while exact match rises by 21 to 34 points. A post-hoc decomposition changes this reading. The optimized prompts elicit reasoning before the required JSON object, and under the 200-token output cap used throughout, 13.9% to 47.0% of responses end before any answer appears. Among parseable outputs, net regression is within 3 points of zero. With a 1,024-token cap, extraction failures disappear, content regression stays below 1 point, and exact match rises further. Two simple gates fail the preregistered criterion. The side effect is narrower than hypothesized: optimized prompts are more accurate and break an explicit output contract, and a lenient target metric does not show the cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13361", "CorpusId": 292830518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "title": "AgentSWE: Can Coding Agents Build the Agent You Actually Want?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13361, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2445720410", "name": "Jia-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374412422", "name": "Hong-Jin Qian"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2321653170", "name": "Jia-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362012611", "name": "Zheng Liu"}], "abstract": "As the production of software passes into the hands of software itself, coding agents are increasingly commissioned to build agents fitted to their users'own workflows. This delegation carries a quiet assumption, that the delivered agent will faithfully serve the requirement it was built for, rather than merely appear to. No existing benchmark tests that assumption. We introduce AgentSWE, a benchmark for agent software engineering spanning three stages of the agent development lifecycle: Creation of a complete agent from a natural-language requirement, Editing of production agent codebases, and Optimization toward a measurable target. Each of its 25 tasks is framed as a commission rather than an exam: the builder iterates against a handful of illustrative cases, while acceptance exercises the frozen submission on held-out cases designed to span the full requirement, grading behavior against requirement-derived rubrics under hard pass/fail gates or, for Optimization, against the source benchmark's own objective. Evaluating a range of coding agents across models and harnesses, we find that none comes close to delivering what a requirement asks for: the gap between what a builder demonstrates and what it delivers is the norm, not the corner case. An accompanying empirical analysis of the failure trajectories identifies recurring failure modes, among them overfitting to the visible cases, hollow runtimes behind code that looks complete, and unsupported claims of completion, each a signal by which coding agents can learn to build the agents they are asked for."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c40827185494c0792885be74093abd3d33c8a30c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11464", "CorpusId": 292738009}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c40827185494c0792885be74093abd3d33c8a30c", "title": "Who Verifies the Verifier? Co-Evolving Inspectable Graders with Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11464, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "102356716", "name": "Xing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2420203852", "name": "Guang-Hui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2309116797", "name": "Ya Cui"}, {"authorId": "2224621614", "name": "Zi-Yuan Li"}, {"authorId": "152525244", "name": "Wei Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2224666655", "name": "Bing Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2299517857", "name": "Pei-Gen He"}], "abstract": "We changed the agent: did it actually get better? Every self-improving agent loop answers this hundreds of times, and every answer comes from a verifier. On open-ended tasks none exists, so the loop is handed a hand-written rubric or a bare LLM judge grading output from a model like itself, inviting reward hacking and shared blind spots. We make the verifier the evolving object: an inspectable expression over small, mostly deterministic drawback detectors, synthesized from clustered failures, gated at birth, and selected for agreement with a ten-item anchored reference set plus consensus over unlabeled outputs, never for the agent's score. On MBPP+ it gains +0.21 held-out agreement over the hand-authored seed composition, on every seed, and ends ahead of the bare LLM judge it contains. One finding should change how co-evolved verifiers are validated: removing the anchor guards collapses the verifier into a vacuous always-pass grader, yet that collapsed verifier trains skills just as well. Downstream task score cannot certify a self-evolved verifier. Score does answer sufficiency, and there an evolved verifier can substitute: Double Ratchet, pairing the verifier with a lifecycle-managed skill loop, retains 88-110% of the lift that ground truth or a rubric buys the same loop, across code generation, enterprise text-to-SQL, and reference-free report generation. When evolved skills gamed the report rubric, an outer judge caught it and one added detector repaired it; the judge itself was wrong until given the task contract."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ca87f22739fb304209138238056f987e05ac8d5e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11613", "CorpusId": 292738580}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ca87f22739fb304209138238056f987e05ac8d5e", "title": "AgentEvolver: System-Wide Self-Evolution Through Task Execution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11613, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2334920503", "name": "Wen-Tao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2402768773", "name": "Fu-Chao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2267697818", "name": "Yi-Lei Zhao"}, {"authorId": "10737491", "name": "Xin-Run Wang"}, {"authorId": "2363859486", "name": "Bo An"}], "abstract": "An agent can complete a task without improving how it works. Turning task experience into reusable capability requires connecting the changed component to its evaluation and subsequent use. We present AgentEvolver, a system for developing capabilities during task execution while keeping the foundation model fixed. Eight entity families expose reusable operations, methods, agents, control flow, interfaces, and supporting state to revision through a common versioned lifecycle. A shared Runtime coordinates ongoing work, while persistent planning and recoverable context preserve task direction and supporting evidence. We evaluate task outcomes on SWE-bench Pro Public and examine capability changes in six application cases. The team reports an 82.08\\% resolution rate with evolution, exceeding its reported baseline without evolution. The cases show retained capabilities entering later website, game, and research work, while also documenting incomplete objectives and an unsuccessful strategy. These findings distinguish improvement in a reusable component from success on the final task. AgentEvolver provides a concrete basis for studying capability accumulation through execution; independent-task transfer and total development cost remain open questions."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "662966f95d6789fd951c4de8a8b51e7a7c8750a5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12601", "CorpusId": 292828961}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/662966f95d6789fd951c4de8a8b51e7a7c8750a5", "title": "Reflective Prompt Optimization of a Deep-Research Agent: A Topic-Disjoint, Multi-Seed Protocol for Measuring Transferable Gains and Prompt-Level Overfitting", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12601, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2326836585", "name": "Elias Lumer"}, {"authorId": "2405112753", "name": "Sahil Sen"}, {"authorId": "2327043802", "name": "V. K. Subbiah"}, {"authorId": "47793506", "name": "Kevin Paul"}], "abstract": "Prompt optimization is an attractive alternative to reinforcement learning for adapting language-model agents, since it is cheap and produces human-readable artifacts. On factual tasks, however, it carries a failure mode that scalar-reward reinforcement learning does not. An optimizer that reads the training examples can write their answers into the prompt, so an in-distribution gain may be memorization rather than transferable behaviour. We apply reflective prompt evolution (GEPA) to the two system prompts of a multi-agent deep-research agent and evaluate it under a topic-disjoint split in which no topic is shared across train, validation, and test. Held-out citation reward rises by 0.079, from 0.702 to 0.781, with a bootstrap 95\\% confidence interval of [0.051, 0.107], and the gain replicates across three seeds (mean 0.107). Nearly half of the winning prompt is a memorized answer key, yet a controlled ablation shows this content earns no reward even on the exact training questions it encodes. The large validation-minus-held-out gap is therefore small-sample selection under a noisy judge, not memorization. Varying the verifier isolates what makes the gain transfer. Decomposed per-dimension feedback is the causal ingredient, and collapsing it to a single scalar reward makes held-out transfer negative. The gain persists under an out-of-family judge and is largely reproduced by a hand-written version of the discovered recipe. Credible evaluation of prompt optimizers therefore requires held-out topics, multiple seeds, and verifier ablations. We release the candidate prompts, lineage diffs, figures, tables, and analysis."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "32a034433c0a6b782f08554e0c7c8855686581ca", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09239", "CorpusId": 292695308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/32a034433c0a6b782f08554e0c7c8855686581ca", "title": "The Winner's Curse in LLM Self-Improvement Loops: Selection Noise, Lock-in, and Acceptance Rules", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09239, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2508874789", "name": "Li-Tao Hu"}, {"authorId": "2119311140", "name": "Yu-Tong Tang"}], "abstract": "Self-improving LLM systems propose changes to themselves and keep those that score better on a small evaluation set. We treat this keep-if-better step as selection under measurement noise, model the correlated errors of the candidates in a single decision, and study empirically what happens when the evaluation set is reused. In runs where Qwen models rewrite their own instructions and every candidate is also scored on 600 held-out items, most proposals after the first are harmful, and the model gives the size of the winner's curse of a generation's best candidate. With a prior from a separate pilot, it matches the average overstatement of first-generation commits in native loops, though not setting by setting. In a pre-registered study, the final selection-set score of greedy loops exceeded held-out accuracy by 13 to 20 points with 16 selection items and by 1 to 5 points with 256. Held-out gains grew with the selection set on TREC but not on GSM8K, and the tested acceptance rules did not beat greedy acceptance over whole runs. Gains measured on the selection set also exceeded held-out gains when a current model refined a competent instruction, and in the validation scores of GEPA and MIPROv2. Scoring the starting and the current instruction on 64 items never used for selection removes the average bias of a loop's reported gain, but single estimates remain off by about 6 points. Self-improvement studies should report held-out gains with their uncertainty."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bce187ccf6ade3cb2ae9ff2919324ef383ead642", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.10426", "CorpusId": 292700991}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bce187ccf6ade3cb2ae9ff2919324ef383ead642", "title": "CoTrace: Data Recipes for Training Terminal Agents with Harness-Model Co-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10426, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2296064274", "name": "Ji-Xuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2383058705", "name": "Jia-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "1557391091", "name": "Qinyuan Ye"}, {"authorId": "100984698", "name": "Yada Pruksachatkun"}, {"authorId": "2287889534", "name": "Haoxiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2275567484", "name": "Jing-Ming Zhuo"}, {"authorId": "2492543093", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2316803927", "name": "Yu-Tong Dai"}, {"authorId": "2286700513", "name": "Jun-Tao Tan"}, {"authorId": "2324805044", "name": "Xiang-Yu Peng"}, {"authorId": "2238207181", "name": "S. Savarese"}, {"authorId": "2292059557", "name": "Ze-Yuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2365503656", "name": "Lian-Hui Qin"}, {"authorId": "2383150796", "name": "Chien-Sheng Wu"}], "abstract": "Terminal-agent capability depends jointly on model weights and the runtime harness that formats prompts, binds tools, and handles error recovery. Existing harness-model co-evolution approaches improve both components, yet often treat trajectories produced during harness search as an undifferentiated replay buffer. This practice overlooks that a trajectory's value for model training depends on the harness under which it was generated. To systematically analyze this interface, we establish an alternating co-evolution framework that decouples harness search and policy training through component-wise promotion decisions. Within this framework, we introduce CoTrace, a harness-aware data recipe that explicitly governs trajectory routing, provenance matching, and curriculum refresh. Under CoTrace, recurring execution failures guide harness synthesis, while policy training is strictly conditioned on verified rollouts matched to the adopted runtime for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or fresh online interactions for reinforcement learning (RL). On the Tmax promotion split, CoTrace advances Qwen3.5-9B from 78 to 88 solved tasks under supervised fine-tuning while an online reinforcement variant reaches 90. Specifically, a compact harness-matched corpus produces steady model gains at substantially lower compute than much larger corpora pooled across sibling harnesses. Furthermore, evaluations on Terminal-Bench 2.1 and SWE-bench Lite show that out-of-distribution transfer depends fundamentally on harness compatibility, where maintaining consistency between training and evaluation runtimes prevents procedural execution breakdowns observed under foreign scaffolds."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1762ae36ce98abdac015ddd2b6e1ac938069fb8d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.07663", "CorpusId": 292663131}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1762ae36ce98abdac015ddd2b6e1ac938069fb8d", "title": "Joint Workflow and Prompt Optimization for User Behavior Simulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07663, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505389963", "name": "Ni-Er Wu"}, {"authorId": "2347574518", "name": "Hong-Zhi Yin"}, {"authorId": "2488355550", "name": "Hui Li"}, {"authorId": "2508150581", "name": "Weiqing Wang Monash University"}, {"authorId": "102913827", "name": "The University of Queensland"}, {"authorId": "102563934", "name": "Xiamen University"}], "abstract": "User behavior simulation is the computational modeling of user interactions within information systems through the use of simulated agents in place of live users. It supports system testing and evaluation, decision-making and forecasting, and user experience design. Existing simulators rely on hand-crafted rules or domain expertise that transfers poorly across tasks. SWORD (Simulation-driven Workflow and Prompt Optimization with Role-based Design) is introduced as a framework that jointly optimizes multi-agent workflow topology and natural-language prompts. It is guided solely by a scalar task metric, without domain initialization or task-specific engineering. The experimental results demonstrate that SWORD achieves statistically significant gains over prompt-only, workflow-only, and staged-optimization baselines under a controlled, identical-backbone comparison. Against the strongest published domain-specific baseline, SWORD further improves accuracy while using a smaller backbone model, substantially less training data, and a very reasonable API cost (\\$4--\\$6 for each dataset). Beyond predictive performance, SWORD autonomously discovers domain-relevant signals, review-sentiment mapping rules and epidemiological decay priors, purely from scalar error feedback, establishing textual gradients as a mechanism for unsupervised feature-importance discovery in user behavior modeling."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "00c47424e8a2836fadaa84af1c35e6d762232d84", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.06511", "CorpusId": 292655586}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/00c47424e8a2836fadaa84af1c35e6d762232d84", "title": "ArtifactArena: Evaluating Models by What They Build in the Physical World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06511, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "150179191", "name": "Kushagra Tiwary"}, {"authorId": "153290081", "name": "David Mayo"}, {"authorId": "2148687825", "name": "Nikhil Behari"}, {"authorId": "2507871439", "name": "Xiang-Zhou Sun"}, {"authorId": "2329188269", "name": "Abdulrahman Alabdulkareem"}, {"authorId": "2287936651", "name": "Isaac R. Galatzer-Levy"}, {"authorId": "143912599", "name": "B. Katz"}, {"authorId": "2328013190", "name": "Brian Cheung"}], "abstract": "To evaluate the frontier, we must measure models not by what they say, but by what they can engineer and build in grounded physical environments. We introduce ArtifactArena, an open-ended platform where models face a physically grounded hardware-software co-design challenge: engineering fully functional robots to compete in a simulated arena. We evaluate a frontier model's zero-shot, verifier guided refinement, and open-ended physical design capabilities through three harnesses that refine their bot artifacts based on text descriptions, physics simulator feedback, and gameplay data. We benchmark these capabilities with an Elo ranking of frontier models derived from head-to-head tournaments between their artifacts. By releasing this framework and tournament infrastructure for ongoing community submissions, we establish a living, non-saturating testbed to continuously measure the expanding limits of open-ended intelligence in the physical world. Please visit https://artifactarena.ai for more information."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a083e479510bc34d8ba5f1f3706a858432e202e8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05923", "CorpusId": 292655286}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a083e479510bc34d8ba5f1f3706a858432e202e8", "title": "VERA: Scaling Verifiable Environments for Agentic co-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05923, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2409174215", "name": "Jun-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505112633", "name": "Yong-Yang Pan"}, {"authorId": "2222322049", "name": "Zhuo-Song Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2319217568", "name": "Dong-Bai Li"}, {"authorId": "2502206556", "name": "Bo Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2453994938", "name": "Xi-Tong Ling"}, {"authorId": "2502613093", "name": "Sheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2372190732", "name": "Han-Rong Ye"}, {"authorId": "2255499417", "name": "Yu-Fan He"}, {"authorId": "2269185997", "name": "Can Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2305603785", "name": "Peng-Fei Guo"}, {"authorId": "2309095535", "name": "Dong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2611242", "name": "Andriy Myronenko"}, {"authorId": "2306069928", "name": "Yu-Yin Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2507869942", "name": "Tian-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2333986617", "name": "Da-Guang Xu"}, {"authorId": "46556781", "name": "Yu-Cheng Tang"}], "abstract": "Competent agents need precise and verifiable environments, such as sandboxes that are resumable at any stage and evolve from observable evidence. However, most long-horizon work exposes how rare these are: for example, an agent in medical research must ground a finding, classify it, and write a report over dozens of dependent steps, yet recent environments score only the outcome. To address the challenges in stable training, we present VERA, which builds such environments at scale and lets agents evolve on them. VERA builds these environments from initial trajectories: an agent writes rubrics, executable checks, a judge verifies each sandbox, and only those that pass enter the training bank. On these environments, VERA alternates between two updates: train the model with rubric rewards, or edit the harness skills. We also create a verifier which gates model checkpoints and harness edits using explicit development-set acceptance criteria. This attribution distinguishes VERA's co-evolution from single-axis baselines: its updates target not only the cause but the outcome. With an open-source corpus of 9,000+ long-horizon verifiable environments, a 9B model paired with its co-evolved agent beats the strongest baseline by 10.3 and 13.0 points in the two domains. At 27B, it surpasses the baseline on AutoCoWorkBench (71.6) and AutoMedBench (80.7), transfers to unseen workflows, and retains general capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "af6cee120b1578f9734436bc938961dac56e2963", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.06563", "CorpusId": 292655287}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/af6cee120b1578f9734436bc938961dac56e2963", "title": "HERA: Harness-Environment Co-Evolution for Reliable Agentic Abstention", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06563, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2110564525", "name": "Hang Luo"}, {"authorId": "2275606374", "name": "Bing-Bing Wen"}, {"authorId": "2506664345", "name": "Guang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2307569999", "name": "Zora Zhiruo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383969505", "name": "Pan Lu"}, {"authorId": "31860505", "name": "Lucy Lu Wang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly capable of acting in complex tool-use environments, yet they often fail to recognize when tasks are infeasible and no valid solution exists. Recent work has formalized this reliability gap as the problem of agentic abstention, and existing approaches typically optimize a model or agent harness against a fixed set of tasks, leading to limited generalization to unseen failure modes. We introduce HERA, a framework for harness-environment co-evolution for agentic abstention. HERA consists of (i) a pipeline to automatically construct verifiable pairs of feasible and infeasible tasks by applying controlled environment mutations that transform solvable tasks into cases requiring abstention, and (ii) a co-evolution procedure in which performance failures on previous tasks are used to drive harness adaptation and generate new execution environments and tasks geared towards previous weaknesses. On held-out evaluation tasks, an evolved harness from HERA improves abstention accuracy from 61.7% to 83.3% while improving feasible-task completion from 68.3% to 76.7%, achieving the highest abstention and feasible-task completion among the compared methods. The resulting best harness transfers across 19 other LLMs, improving abstention accuracy by 15.3 percentage points on average without any model-specific optimization, and enabling smaller models to match the performance of more powerful models at an estimated 85% lower cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "177f91efe7c09e44cf121b111048343aca568181", "externalIds": {"PubMedCentral": "13638071", "DOI": "10.2196/106133", "CorpusId": 292659361, "PubMed": "42832768"}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/177f91efe7c09e44cf121b111048343aca568181", "title": "AI-Based Structured Information Extraction From Synthetic Nursing Handover Transcripts: Comparative Evaluation of Large Language Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.2196/106133", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13638071, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Medicine"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2290049948", "name": "Aaron Conway"}, {"authorId": "88515003", "name": "Adriana Hada"}, {"authorId": "2507908732", "name": "Jessica Schluter"}, {"authorId": "2352493788", "name": "Hui Grace Xu"}, {"authorId": "2507907895", "name": "Dan Lowden"}, {"authorId": "2310102406", "name": "Tim Miller"}, {"authorId": "2229749251", "name": "Ken Donald"}, {"authorId": "2272496133", "name": "Andrew Teodorczuk"}], "abstract": "Abstract Background Clinical handover is the process during which responsibility and accountability for care are transferred between clinicians. AI has the potential to improve the reliability and completeness of clinical handover by helping clinicians detect predefined content areas that have been communicated, identify explicit information gaps, and prompt clarification before responsibility is transferred. Objective This study evaluated the performance of several large language models and prompt optimization strategies for structured information extraction of synthetic nursing handover transcripts. Methods Two registered nurses independently annotated a dataset of 203 synthetic handover transcripts to produce consensus labels for information extraction tasks. Tasks included (1) labeling spans of text into SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) categories, (2) content detection to determine if specific pieces of information were communicated, and (3) labeling spans of text that communicated information using uncertain terms that included a subtask for identifying unknown facts. Baseline and Genetic-Pareto (GEPA)\u2013optimized prompts were compared for the GPT-5.2, GPT-5-nano, and MedGemma 27B large language models. Additionally, the LangExtract framework was evaluated for span-extraction tasks. Results The GPT-5.2\u2013optimized model achieved a micro\u2013F1-score of 0.85 (95% CI 0.83\u20100.88) for content detection, an absolute improvement of +0.08 compared with the matched baseline. GPT-5-nano also performed better after optimization for content detection (micro\u2013F1-score 0.81, 95% CI 0.78\u20100.84), suggesting that this structured task was not limited to the highest-capacity model. For SBAR span extraction, GPT-5.2 with prompt optimization achieved a micro\u2013F1-score of 0.76 (95% CI 0.72\u20100.79), improving by +0.24 compared with baseline and exceeding LangExtract; GPT-5-nano also improved to a micro\u2013F1-score of 0.69 (95% CI 0.66\u20100.72). Broad uncertainty-span extraction remained comparatively weak despite prompt optimization (micro\u2013F1-score 0.41, 95% CI 0.33\u20100.48; absolute improvement +0.06). In contrast, explicit unknown-fact extraction was more accurate with GPT-5.2 (micro\u2013F1-score 0.84, 95% CI 0.63\u20101.00), GPT-5-nano (micro\u2013F1-score, 0.84 95% CI 0.63\u20101.00), and MedGemma 27B (micro\u2013F1-score 0.80, 95% CI 0.63\u20101.00). Genetic-Pareto\u2013optimized prompts outperformed the LangExtract approach across each span-extraction task. Conclusions Prompt optimization improved matched-model point estimates, with the highest performance observed for predefined content detection and SBAR span extraction. Broad uncertainty extraction remained less accurate than the narrower unknown-fact task. These technical results do not establish clinical effectiveness, safety, or readiness for real-time use. Validation using authentic nursing handover communication and prospective evaluation in clinical workflows are required before clinical application."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "52ab630fd1f3470d356bc67b988a182d75bce95a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.04824", "CorpusId": 292654780}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/52ab630fd1f3470d356bc67b988a182d75bce95a", "title": "Agent Behavior as Code: Efficient and Robust LLM Agents with Programmatic Specifications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04824, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2287304868", "name": "Peng Qi"}, {"authorId": "2078685482", "name": "Chunliang Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2386554946", "name": "Gang Li"}, {"authorId": "19263810", "name": "Fa-Bian Chan"}, {"authorId": "2324841935", "name": "Cheng Chang"}, {"authorId": "2385476030", "name": "Ignacio Cases"}, {"authorId": "2507871240", "name": "Will Lu"}], "abstract": "AI agents based on foundation models (FMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities to perform complex open-ended tasks. However, they face some common challenges in practice: (a) agent behavior can deviate drastically even for semantically similar tasks, leading to catastrophically propagated errors; (b) high cost and latency due to FM calls, repeated in full whenever a task recurs with different inputs; (c) FMs'limited context and instruction following capability confine how well agents manage the ever-growing execution context and follow complex plans. We introduce $\\textbf{A}$gent $\\textbf{B}$ehavior as $\\textbf{C}$ode $\\textbf{Agent}$ (ABCAgent), which uses a symbolic program (e.g., Python code with potential neural functions) to fully specify the agent's behavior at runtime, with a powerful FM agent editing that program for flexibility. Behavior is thus specified without premature variable binding, and its execution is deterministic. We evaluate ABCAgent on six agent benchmarks, two of which we construct to test how well a derived program generalizes to variants of the task it was written for. ABCAgent matches a model-matched neural agent on GAIA and augmented GAIA, and surpasses it where robustness and long control flows matter: 98.3% against 97.3% on GSM-Symbolic ($p = 0.001$), 71.9% against 47.4% $\\mathrm{Pass}^4$ on the telecom domain of $\\tau^2$-bench ($p = 0.0001$), and more records written correctly at every loop length on our control-flow-augmented WorkArena benchmark. For more parametric task families, ABCAgent is also significantly superior in efficiency. Without authoring a new program, ABCAgent solves 92.6% of GSM-Symbolic instances and 20.1% of augmented GAIA variants, which yields $5.2\\times$ lower latency and $7.0\\times$ lower cost on GSM-Symbolic, 19% lower cost on augmented GAIA, and $9.5\\times$ lower agent latency on $\\tau^2$-telecom."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1fe9cca510e284892cd77dd9d1d7e5ab42a8ef34", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02687", "CorpusId": 292587080}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1fe9cca510e284892cd77dd9d1d7e5ab42a8ef34", "title": "Decoupling Memory from Context: Structured Memory for Token-Efficient Test-Time Continual Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02687, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2268493241", "name": "Yehya Farhat"}, {"authorId": "2295340103", "name": "Michael Desmond"}, {"authorId": "2126894228", "name": "Anastasios Kyrillidis"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in enterprise, scientific, and medical applications, where agents must incorporate domain-specific knowledge and adapt from experience. Context engineering offers a practical alternative to weight updates by improving model behavior through instructions, strategies, and evidence supplied at inference time. However, adapting context online typically requires a costly trial-and-error process, while queries are often processed independently, preventing useful experience from carrying forward. Memory systems address this limitation by retaining information across interactions, but approaches that continually append information to a shared context face increasing token costs, context-window limits, and performance degradation as the context expands. We introduce a unified formulation of context optimization and show that an agent memory system update can be interpreted as an optimization update procedure over the model's context. This perspective attempts to provide a principled framework for studying memory design and its efficiency. We then propose GraphMemory, a lightweight graph-based memory that accumulates, refines, organizes, and connects reusable strategies. For each query, GraphMemory retrieves only the relevant subgraph, enabling online context adaptation without exposing the model to the entire memory. Under bounded retrieval, the amount of retrieved memory remains constant as the number of processed examples grows. Experiments show that GraphMemory achieves competitive downstream performance while using approximately 81-85% fewer memory-construction tokens than our baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2bf87e21b4bd17f60d8ac89332c322feeef1ff70", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.03123", "CorpusId": 292590448}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2bf87e21b4bd17f60d8ac89332c322feeef1ff70", "title": "Foresight: planning future perception in streaming VLMs without retraining", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03123, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455711135", "name": "Ashok Prasad Neupane"}, {"authorId": "2507129477", "name": "Dipan Bartaula"}, {"authorId": "2507129743", "name": "Ankit Belbase"}, {"authorId": "41071419", "name": "Saugat Adhikari"}, {"authorId": "2195276543", "name": "Samip Ghimire"}, {"authorId": "2507129444", "name": "Saroj Poudel"}, {"authorId": "2078892", "name": "Binod Bhattarai"}, {"authorId": "35268081", "name": "D. Paudel"}], "abstract": "Existing streaming vision-language models (VLMs) continuously perceive and reason over visual streams, but their computational pathways remain fixed throughout inference. Consequently, they cannot adapt computation to evolving scene dynamics, where different future events demand different levels and forms of perception. We show that streaming VLMs inherently possess the ability to anticipate the immediate future, and leverage this capability to dynamically configure future computation in a training-free manner. Realizing such anticipatory computation, however, is very challenging: future anticipation must be sufficiently reliable to guide computation, planning must run concurrently with streaming inference, and online reconfiguration must incur negligible overhead. To address these challenges, we introduce FORESIGHT, a dual-stream architecture comprising two Siamese LLMs with shared weights, input encoders, and KV cache. The first LLM continuously processes incoming tokens, while the second runs ahead of the stream to anticipate future context, plan future computation, and generate task responses without interrupting streaming inference. Each plan decides when to reason next, what to check then, and how densely to sample, keeping transient evidence separate from persistent control. The resulting computation plan is executed online through an efficient reconfiguration protocol with schemaguided decoding and lightweight diff-based updates, enabling dynamic adaptation with low overhead. With a frozen Qwen3-VL-8B backbone, FORESIGHT achieves 23.0 mean joint F1 on OmniPro Online evaluation beating strongest trained baseline by 9.5%, while improving the backbone by 6.7 on StreamingBench and 15.4 on OVO-Bench, with the largest gain of 18.7 when evidence arrives later in the video stream. Our source code will be made publicly available."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.01073", "CorpusId": 292521530}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "title": "Safety Must Survive Self-Improvement: Why Failures Persist and How Agents Recover", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01073, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2221081346", "name": "Yun-Bei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2221231969", "name": "Janet Wang"}, {"authorId": "2214534099", "name": "Saiyue Lyu"}, {"authorId": "152988336", "name": "Ying-Qiang Ge"}, {"authorId": "2087743517", "name": "Kaiqu Liang"}, {"authorId": "2278326150", "name": "Zi-Jian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2322749934", "name": "Chandan K. Reddy"}, {"authorId": "2282741360", "name": "J. Hamm"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) allows agents to carry useful changes across generations. Maintaining safety across these generations involves both preventing unsafe behavior from persisting and enabling recovery when failures occur. We study these challenges through a controlled testbed of stateful authorization tasks, where fixed LLM editors optimize executable agent components and independent traces record their effects. Paired interventions separate which revisions pass validation, which program continues running, and which program the editor revises next. After a new authorization dependency invalidates previously tested optimizations, historical scores preserve the same unsafe programs in 22 of 48 framework histories despite a correct alternative in every affected archive. Refreshing scores restores correctness on the original suite, with residual failures on independently composed tests. Failures also persist under an unchanged contract when all proposals are rejected and the failed incumbent remains active. Starting from shared failures, editing the initial implementation instead of the failed one improves recovery, although the advantage varies across editors. Full validation and validated rollback end with fully correct programs in the core trajectory study while retaining over 43% deployment savings. Preserving agent safety requires checking what will run under current conditions and choosing which implementation to edit next."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b01e154957b584cecea32b5ceabae89ddcd7a4f8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02001", "CorpusId": 292525312}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b01e154957b584cecea32b5ceabae89ddcd7a4f8", "title": "Mingbird: A Local-First Agent Harness Enabling Small Open Models to Complete Real Tasks", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506681013", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2489803684", "name": "Ting-Bin Huang"}], "abstract": "Small open-weight models (2-9B) run on ordinary laptops, but under cloud-scale agent harnesses they rarely complete real tasks: tool prefill overflows the context, self-correction diverges, tool demonstrations loop, and tasks are silently abandoned. We present evidence, from a controlled single-machine comparison and one third-party benchmark, that a substantial share of these failures is attributable to the harness rather than the model. We introduce Mingbird, a local-first agent harness for Windows and Ollama whose ten mechanisms compensate point-by-point for small-model failure forms, three of them representative: a byte-level net-zero prefill budget, a finish gate that re-reads the task before accepting completion, and signature-level loop detection. On LRAB, a controlled comparison holding machine, models, budgets, and scoring fixed (4 harnesses $\\times$ 4 open models (2B-35B) $\\times$ 18 real tasks, deterministic artifact scoring), Mingbird reaches 0.886 overall against 0.631 (goose), 0.479 (opencode), and 0.405 (agent-mini), with all 288 cells published; on $\\tau^2$-bench (278 tasks, three arms, one protocol) it totals 0.856 against 0.791 and 0.737; and a frontier-model probe on the same 18 tasks spans 0.997 to 0.478 across harnesses, with well-formed scaffolds staying within 0.072 of each other. A leave-one-mechanism-out ablation is reported as directional only: same-night replications of the same arm move its mean by up to 0.069, the size of every nominal single-trial delta, and the one batch-matched comparison (full mechanism stack versus text re-read alone) gives the executable completion guards a paired +0.10 across three replications. The evidence carries stated limits: a self-built benchmark, a single machine, and single-trial scoring."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "08a3c95a79decd8ec93d0ebae2626b561f296b08", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.00416", "CorpusId": 292521016}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/08a3c95a79decd8ec93d0ebae2626b561f296b08", "title": "Benchmarking Prompt Optimization of Large Language Models With Chess", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00416, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "26418330", "name": "Timoth\u00e9e Lesort"}, {"authorId": "2506318761", "name": "Alejandra L\u00f3pez de Aberasturi-G\u00f3mez"}, {"authorId": "2346112663", "name": "Tristan Karch"}, {"authorId": "19317494", "name": "Tom Veniat"}, {"authorId": "2365040765", "name": "Philippe Modard"}, {"authorId": "2351685544", "name": "Karl Tuyls"}, {"authorId": "8905591", "name": "Ludovic Denoyer"}], "abstract": "Evaluating large language models becomes increasingly challenging as their capabilities advance: benchmarks can saturate, public test sets risk contamination, and assessing harder tasks can require expensive grading or execution infrastructure. These challenges are amplified in automatic prompt optimization (APO), where evaluation is repeated throughout the search for better prompts. Studying APO therefore requires a benchmark that is cheap and deterministic to score, hard enough to leave room for improvement, and renewable as models evolve. We introduce a chess benchmark built from 1,118 Lichess puzzles to study APO for frozen LLMs: we optimize their prompts without updating their model weights. Chess combines inexpensive exact-match scoring, engine-based evaluation of alternative moves, and a renewable supply of problems with adjustable difficulty. Unlike evaluations that report only success on isolated test items, the benchmark also connects puzzle-solving gains to short game-play rollouts within the same domain. We use it to evaluate six APO algorithms on eight target models, measuring not only baseline strength but also how much each model responds to optimization and whether optimized prompts transfer across models and to game play. Chess is thus a well-suited benchmark for APO: it is (i) challenging, as even the strongest evaluated model, Gemini 3.5 Flash (used as the meta-model), solves only about 55\\% of puzzles; (ii) discriminative, revealing gains, unchanged performance, and regressions across methods and models; (iii) renewable, with fresh puzzles to reduce contamination risk and adjustable difficulty to maintain headroom as models improve; and (iv) affordable, as the complete study runs for around \\$800. We release the puzzles, optimization and evaluation code, and dataset-renewal scripts (https://github.com/imec-ailabs/Automatic-Prompt-Optimization-with-Chess)."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eea051efe03f38966b2ff5e43a5acf26dacab65e", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-40361", "ArXiv": "2609.40361", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.40361", "CorpusId": 292486682}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eea051efe03f38966b2ff5e43a5acf26dacab65e", "title": "Ranking-Aware Prompt Optimization for Multimodal Clinical Diagnosis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40361, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2458355539", "name": "Tian Xia"}, {"authorId": "2302812553", "name": "Ming-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2257367768", "name": "Yi-Qing Liang"}, {"authorId": "2364299153", "name": "Lai-Xi Shi"}, {"authorId": "2271351225", "name": "Jia-Yun Wang"}], "abstract": "Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are rapidly advancing clinical diagnosis, yet their adaptation pipelines remain anchored to accuracy-based objectives. Clinical data are heavily class-imbalanced: a constant-majority predictor can score above 90% accuracy while being clinically useless. We therefore evaluate and optimize for AUROC, a threshold-free score that ranks positives above negatives and is invariant to class balance. We focus on prompt optimization in MLLMs. Reflective methods such as GEPA use a binary scores matrix with one row per evaluation instance and one column per candidate prompt; cells record per-instance correctness, so the column average is accuracy and drives candidate selection. We introduce pair-level Pareto prompt evolution (Ranking-PE), which replaces each correctness row with a pairwise-ordering row over (positive, negative) instance pairs: the cell is 1 if the candidate scores the positive higher than the paired negative. The column average then equals empirical AUROC (by the Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney identity). We apply this swap at all three layers the prompt evolution search reads from - the scores matrix that decides Pareto dominance, the per-example feedback to the reflection LM, and final candidate selection - at no extra model calls and with no surrogate loss. Across three diseases on MIMIC, accuracy-based prompt evolution can degrade ranking; Ranking-PE reverses this, beating the accuracy-based recipe by +5.8 AUROC pp on fine-tuned Qwen3-VL-8B and +16.2 pp on MedGemma-4B. Ablations examine each design component and show that a medical-grade visual backbone - via vision-encoder-tuned SFT or medical pretraining - is a prerequisite that prompt search cannot replace - our recipe extends reflective prompt evolution from text-only data to multimodal clinical decision-making."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fa6f37a97ae12af343d3867bb6735d2d9d59cf7c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.38762", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-38762", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.38762", "CorpusId": 292477109}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fa6f37a97ae12af343d3867bb6735d2d9d59cf7c", "title": "Adaptive-GEPA: Make Your Harness Fit Heterogeneous Requests", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38762, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2399156405", "name": "Tian-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2296023267", "name": "Yasi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2266240014", "name": "Rui-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2329112407", "name": "Xin-Ran Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2363567316", "name": "Tao-Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2257010071", "name": "Ming-Yuan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Reflective optimizers such as GEPA improve language model prompts from execution traces and evaluator feedback; full-program extensions can also rewrite tools and control flow. In practice, a user hands the same endpoint heterogeneous requests whose effective solutions require different tools, reasoning modes, and control flow. Optimizing one shared program leaves this division of work implicit in source-code search, while optimizing a separate program per request family fixes it beforehand. We introduce Adaptive-GEPA, which learns both how to divide requests and how to solve them. It evolves a router and a library of specialist programs under one search budget. The router's instructions, each specialist's description, and its program code are plain, human-readable text, edited from feedback. To combine branches, it aligns specialists by the requests they handle and inherits descriptions together with programs. On a fixed mixture of four task families, the reported Qwen3-8B run evolves four experts without supplying family labels to the router or reflection model; its routing matches the task partition on all 651 test requests. Its family-mean test score (x100) rises from 52.6 to 70.6, compared with 62.5 for GEPA's full-program adapter and 54.0 for GRPO at a nominal budget of 18,000 scored calls. These counts do not equate total compute. Figure 1 summarizes the learning curves, final test scores, and routing agreement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5c6f8648c3b324b4daf25c79a12f116691cc5791", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.00704", "CorpusId": 292521713}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5c6f8648c3b324b4daf25c79a12f116691cc5791", "title": "SkillSpec: Consensus-Gated Agent Skill Evolution via Representation Specialization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00704, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2467997082", "name": "Huan-Cheng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2516254148", "name": "Xi Sun"}, {"authorId": "2506338691", "name": "Zhao-Qiong Huang"}, {"authorId": "2510656138", "name": "Shen-Yang Huang"}, {"authorId": "2220891782", "name": "Shreya Singhal"}, {"authorId": "2301157480", "name": "Jing-Wen Lu"}], "abstract": "Natural-language skills are textual procedural memories through which large language model (LLM) agents retain reusable task knowledge without updating model weights. Existing methods typically treat skills as either static artifacts or monolithic documents optimized using aggregate validation scores as feedback. However, representing a skill as a monolithic document restricts optimization to its textual content, without explicitly modeling the structure through which procedural knowledge is retrieved and executed. We identify a key distinction between learning what knowledge to retain and determining how to organize it: textual updates should first be validated through execution evidence, after which the retained knowledge should be structured according to its procedural dependencies and retrieval requirements. To this end, we introduce SkillSpec, a two-phase framework comprising consensus-gated evolution and representation specialization. In the consensus-gated phase, complementary editing intents generate complete candidate skills. An update is committed only when paired evaluations reach consensus, requiring sufficient overall improvement and non-negative aggregate paired gain in every repeated evaluation. In the specialization phase, signals of process and redundancy sensitivity derived from the full optimization trajectory, including accepted and rejected candidates, guide the selection of a flat, graph, or hybrid representation.Across six benchmarks and three target language models, SkillSpec improves average success rate over SkillOpt by 6.89%, averaged across the three models. These results demonstrate that reliable skill evolution and representation specialization address complementary objectives: deciding what knowledge to retain and how to structure it for inference."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4f0e00df82745cef42138bf949dce8afa537bab6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.00650", "CorpusId": 292521917}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4f0e00df82745cef42138bf949dce8afa537bab6", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Rules for AI Coding Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.00650, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2293232142", "name": "Zheng-Yuan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2486137949", "name": "Reachal Wang"}, {"authorId": "2293409460", "name": "Yue-Peng Hu"}, {"authorId": "2363881123", "name": "Yu-Pu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2260844526", "name": "Yu-Qi Jia"}, {"authorId": "144516687", "name": "N. Gong"}], "abstract": "The performance of AI coding agents is highly dependent on their underlying coding rules. However, existing coding rules are typically hand-crafted and fixed, making the process labor-intensive and often suboptimal. In this work, we propose RuleEvolve, a self-evolving framework for coding rules. RuleEvolve maintains a pool of candidate coding rules and iteratively improves them. In each iteration, it employs an LLM-powered mutator module to generate variants from existing candidates, and then uses a judge module to evaluate these variants and update the pool with the best-performing ones. Extensive evaluations across two coding-agent frameworks, four backbone LLMs, and three benchmarks demonstrate that RuleEvolve outperforms both manual engineering and existing prompt optimization baselines in terms of functional correctness of the generated code, code length, and/or generation cost (e.g., tokens used)."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f08a93fa391b5e32e0547f199a87c176fa1815d9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-36626", "ArXiv": "2609.36626", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.36626", "CorpusId": 292441596}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f08a93fa391b5e32e0547f199a87c176fa1815d9", "title": "Semantic Projection for Continual Self-Evolution of Language Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36626, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340081677", "name": "Zi-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2303640667", "name": "Jun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2497786624", "name": "Li-Xu Wang"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents increasingly rely on persistent natural-language skills to adapt beyond their frozen model parameters. When a shared skill is repeatedly revised from a non-stationary, heterogeneous task stream, however, improvements for new tasks can overwrite procedures needed for earlier ones. In continual learning, Orthogonal Gradient Descent (OGD) addresses analogous interference by projecting a new-task gradient onto a subspace that locally preserves prior predictions. Natural-language skill revisions, however, have neither gradients nor a canonical vector space in which such a projection can be performed. We introduce \\emph{Semantic-Scope Projected Evolution} (SSPE), which transfers the functional principle of gradient projection from parameter space to behavior space. SSPE treats an unconstrained skill revision as a proposed update, identifies acquired capabilities with which it may interfere, and uses the observed gains and regressions to construct a compatible revision rather than merely rejecting the update. This enables one shared skill to evolve across latent and recurring task contexts without exposing semantic domain identities to the evolution model. Across controlled synthetic streams and heterogeneous real-agent benchmarks, SSPE improves final cross-domain competence and mitigates forgetting relative to strong skill-evolution baselines. The evolved skill also retains the strongest average performance after transfer to a different executor model. These results establish semantic projection as a promising principle for stable and adaptive self evolution of language agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0cf4de820f9a81b4f5ce7aba205d10d199cccfdf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.37105", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-37105", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.37105", "CorpusId": 292474387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0cf4de820f9a81b4f5ce7aba205d10d199cccfdf", "title": "VACE: Validation-Gated Alternating Co-Evolution of Agent Models and Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37105, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505721449", "name": "Jie-Xing Qi"}, {"authorId": "2448361227", "name": "Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2328357685", "name": "Jun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2228431017", "name": "Qi-Chen Huang"}, {"authorId": "2274562434", "name": "Shao-Hua Hu"}, {"authorId": "2498905222", "name": "Zhan-Cui Dang"}, {"authorId": "2186808656", "name": "Guo-Hua Chen"}, {"authorId": "2304173133", "name": "Rui Yang"}, {"authorId": "2354630314", "name": "Wen Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2472162107", "name": "Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2486972744", "name": "Tao Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2463564950", "name": "Fang-Ming Li"}], "abstract": "Language model agents can be improved by updating their model weights or refining the harness that guides task execution. These components are coupled: weight updates change how the model uses the harness, while harness updates change the trajectories used for training. We propose VACE, Validation-Gated Alternating CoEvolution, which alternates agentic reinforcement learning with trajectory-driven harness refinement. After each RL stage, VACE reuses the collected trajectories to propose a harness revision and evaluates the incumbent and candidate with the updated model held fixed. The candidate guides subsequent training only if it improves validation performance. With Qwen3.5-9B, VACE achieves 45.26% test accuracy on OfficeQA and a mean partial-credit score of 75.19% on AutomationBench, exceeding weight-only RL by 6.43 and 9.09 percentage points and ungated alternation by 4.59 and 6.95 points, respectively. Across 44 harness proposals, 17 reduce validation performance at the updated checkpoint and are rejected before subsequent RL training, highlighting the importance of validation gating."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "597a2f31ebdedf42e44062d2ae70d81e9c7d7f62", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.37834", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-37834", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.37834", "CorpusId": 292474583}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/597a2f31ebdedf42e44062d2ae70d81e9c7d7f62", "title": "Mixture of Self-Improving Branches For Agent Harness Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37834, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2309801263", "name": "Hao-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2387206689", "name": "Yu-Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2505783308", "name": "Zi-Hao Lin"}, {"authorId": "2328023239", "name": "Yi-Fan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2405641662", "name": "Bo Peng"}, {"authorId": "2330281890", "name": "Ming-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2323788930", "name": "Xiang-Jun Fan"}, {"authorId": "2340399373", "name": "Li-Zhu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization provides a practical setting for recursive self-improvement (RSI), where agent-generated modifications inform subsequent changes through execution feedback. Recent work such as Meta-Harness implements this process through iterative code generation and evaluation, but retains a fixed development set and proposal policy. These constraints channel evolution along a single search trajectory, increasing the risk of converging to a local optimum. We make the improvement process itself adaptive by organizing search into branches with evolving development subsets and proposal policies. Each branch retains development cases solved by more of its leading harnesses than by those of other branches, drops cases solved by every leading harness across all branches, and revises its proposal policy using its own search history. To deploy the resulting complementary harnesses, we propose a router to select one development-selected branch head for each new input before execution. Across mathematical reasoning and agentic coding benchmarks, our system achieves relative improvements over Meta-Harness of 34.8% on Olympiad-level mathematical reasoning, 11.6% on Terminal-Bench 2.0, and 3.8% on SWE-bench Lite, with harness selection and router configuration based solely on development data. These results show that evolving branch objectives and proposal policies can yield complementary harnesses whose strengths a router combines without access to test outcomes."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cb609fd5cc26ac222b1771000ec9173486055073", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.38660", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-38660", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.38660", "CorpusId": 292477041}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cb609fd5cc26ac222b1771000ec9173486055073", "title": "Breaking Babel: A Self-Evolving Multi-Agent System for Long-Form Subtitle Translation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38660, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2304364004", "name": "Hai-Bo Jin"}, {"authorId": "2506238673", "name": "Xin-Jie Li"}, {"authorId": "50178247", "name": "N. Sadoughi"}, {"authorId": "2499575686", "name": "Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2455183223", "name": "Yi-Bo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2340402124", "name": "Zhu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510549287", "name": "Yu-Zong Liu"}], "abstract": "Long-form subtitle translation requires reasoning over discourse and cultural context spanning episodes or entire series, while maintaining consistent terminology and style. Existing single-LLM methods are largely sentence-level, and multi-agent systems often use static workflows that do not adapt to scene complexity or production context. We propose SMART, a Self-evolving Multi-Agent system for long-foRm subtitle Translation. During test-time training, SMART builds persistent series-level memory and translates a subset of sentences through a dynamic router and Mixture-of-Agents layer with tools for terminology verification, subtitle constraint validation, and contextual retrieval. A judge-refiner loop scores candidates and uses textual critiques to update agent prompts and routing policies without retraining the underlying LLMs. During test-time inference, the evolved configuration translates the remaining series. We also introduce Subtitle Arena, covering 14 genres, 2--198 episodes per series, production years 1959--2023, and 15 target locales, together with SubMQM, a subtitle-adapted MQM framework with seven dimensions and 19 error categories. SMART achieves the best overall MQM score in all 15 Subtitle Arena directions, reducing average penalty by 6.9% over the strongest competing agent system. On a public benchmark, MuSC, SMART obtains the best model result across all 4 language pairs. SMART also achieves the best result in human evaluation with an overall score of 4.50/5."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9a0e68a59b60f99987a8ab4edaa1acb0c95b744b", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-38516", "ArXiv": "2609.38516", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.38516", "CorpusId": 292477423}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9a0e68a59b60f99987a8ab4edaa1acb0c95b744b", "title": "From Solo to Social Learning: Characterizing Recursive Social Improvement in LLMs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38516, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-29", "authors": [{"authorId": "2355871569", "name": "Kunal Jha"}, {"authorId": "1390033240", "name": "Max Kleiman-Weiner"}, {"authorId": "3106683", "name": "Natasha Jaques"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) can now improve themselves by revising the instructions they follow, and LLM agents are increasingly orchestrated to work together on complex problems. However, self-improvement methods typically optimize one system at a time, and multi-agent frameworks often have every model work toward a shared goal. We ask a different question. When each agent pursues its own reward, can self-improving LLMs learn from one another well enough to improve the whole population? We call this capability recursive social improvement. We study populations that revise skill files and choose whether, when, and whom to copy from. Independent search, learning from peers, and acting all share one token budget. In controlled environments, established social-learning algorithms benefit from peers, but three LLMs do not. They earn less reward per token than solo learners, and explore too narrowly or run out of tokens before acting. We then let the models write and revise their own skills. Observing peers changes how they improve, helping one model find useful skills sooner and another spend less on private search. Neither, however, outperforms independent learners at the same cost. Skills are copied, revised, and passed on, so one discovery can seed further search. Yet these exchanges concentrate the population around fewer independent discoveries. Together, these results show that LLMs can make learning more efficient by copying from peers, but not yet more effective."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "091cda10eba24a24112b521ce24aa15265e0c71d", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1145/3830418.3843874", "CorpusId": 292412776}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/091cda10eba24a24112b521ce24aa15265e0c71d", "title": "Skill VM: Write Once, Run Everywhere Efficiently", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3830418.3843874", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1145/3830418.3843874?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1145/3830418.3843874, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2223238291", "name": "Le Chen"}, {"authorId": "2123111601", "name": "Er-Hu Feng"}, {"authorId": "2348516202", "name": "Yu-Bin Xia"}, {"authorId": "2285722270", "name": "Hai-Bo Chen"}], "abstract": "LLM agents increasingly adopt skills as a reusable unit of composition. While skills are shared across diverse agent platforms, current systems treat them as raw context, causing the same skill to behave inconsistently for different agents. This fragility undermines skill portability and execution efficiency. To address this challenge, we analyze 118,000 skills and draw inspiration from traditional compiler design. We treat skills as code and LLMs as heterogeneous processors. To make portability actionable, we decompose a skill's requirements into a set of primitive capabilities, and measure how well each model-harness pair supports them. Based on these capability profiles, we propose SkillVM, a compilation and runtime system designed for portable and efficient skill execution. At compile time, SkillVM performs capability-based compilation, environment binding, and concurrency extraction. At runtime, SkillVM applies JIT code solidification and adaptive recompilation for performance optimization. We evaluate SkillVM across ten LLMs of varying scales and four agent harnesses, covering SkillsBench and representative skill tasks. Results demonstrate that SkillVM significantly improves task completion rates across different models and environments while reducing token consumption by up to 40%. In terms of performance, SkillVM achieves up to 3.2\u00d7 speedup with enhanced parallelism, and 19-50\u00d7 latency reduction through code solidification."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "55404eeaa6154851769bb993ddf12a519ef3085e", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34540", "ArXiv": "2609.34540", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34540", "CorpusId": 292434462}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/55404eeaa6154851769bb993ddf12a519ef3085e", "title": "APOLO: Automatic Prompt Optimization for Ontology Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34540, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283006540", "name": "Huu Tan Mai"}, {"authorId": "2354245585", "name": "Roman Kochnev"}, {"authorId": "10000157", "name": "C. Chu"}, {"authorId": "47665464", "name": "Lukas Lange"}, {"authorId": "2279997583", "name": "Heiko Paulheim"}, {"authorId": "2257283625", "name": "Daria Stepanova"}], "abstract": "Ontology Learning (OL) from text has advanced with the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs), but it remains challenging due to the limited availability of annotated training data and the difficulty of adapting LLMs to perform OL effectively. We address this via APOLO - Automatic Prompt Optimization for Ontology Learning, by casting OL as an explicit prompt optimization problem over LLM modules. To obtain training data, we employ a multi-agent system that generates text-ontology pairs from existing expert-curated ontologies. We then propose two ontology learner architectures: a greedy and an autoregressive learner, and optimize both using GEPA, a greedy evolutionary prompt optimizer built on DSPy. Experiments on two ontologies - a biomedical (DOID) and a plant ontology (PO) show consistent improvements after optimization across nearly all model and mode combinations, with autoregressive learners achieving the largest gains. Our results demonstrate that prompt optimization is a viable and lightweight alternative to fine-tuning for OL, and that the autoregressive formulation better captures ontological structure than the greedy approach."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bede38b0266942c936a82aa17cf98c3c98761084", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.34397", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34397", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34397", "CorpusId": 292435224}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bede38b0266942c936a82aa17cf98c3c98761084", "title": "SkillFocus: Evolving Agent Skills via Capability Decomposition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34397, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505387363", "name": "Ning Wang"}, {"authorId": "2434977572", "name": "Z. Gong"}, {"authorId": "2185835781", "name": "Bing-Dong Li"}, {"authorId": "2289681804", "name": "Peng Yang"}, {"authorId": "2398807620", "name": "Ai-Min Zhou"}], "abstract": "Agent skill evolution seeks to improve reusable procedural guidance for large language model (LLM) agents through iterative revision. Existing methods base each revision mainly on execution trajectories or feedback, leaving recurring behavioral requirements across tasks implicit and tying revision to the behavior of the current skill. We introduce SkillFocus, which decomposes recurring task requirements into a capability space that remains fixed as the skill evolves, separating what tasks require from how the current skill behaves. SkillFocus maps current task outcomes to this space to identify the capability that leaves the most tasks unresolved, then uses that capability to determine what to revise and which evidence to use. Across four benchmarks spanning heterogeneous tasks, SkillFocus achieves the best held-out accuracy on all four, outperforming the strongest competing result by 5.7 points on average while using 24\\% fewer evolution tokens on average than the closest iterative baseline. Controlled studies further show that capabilities derived from recurring task requirements outperform task-semantic and execution-derived alternatives, while randomizing task--capability assignments reduces final accuracy by up to 20.2 points. Matching evidence to the selected capability increases candidate gain by 4.4 points under prioritized revision."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7df4d9b943285ee360df876912451d46a99ccb7f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.35760", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35760", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35760", "CorpusId": 292434853}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7df4d9b943285ee360df876912451d46a99ccb7f", "title": "TokenCast: Forecasting Token Consumption During LLM Agent Execution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35760, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2422994606", "name": "Chao-Qian Ouyang"}, {"authorId": "2359688261", "name": "Ling Yue"}, {"authorId": "2316381583", "name": "Li-Bin Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2283536492", "name": "Hanghui Guo"}, {"authorId": "2409962236", "name": "Sheng-Xiang Xu"}, {"authorId": "2393057110", "name": "Yi-Shu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2310570226", "name": "Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2307081545", "name": "Jian Yin"}, {"authorId": "2374104171", "name": "Shao-Wu Pan"}, {"authorId": "2345012341", "name": "Shi-Min Di"}], "abstract": "When a large language model (LLM) agent executes the same task, token consumption can vary by over an order of magnitude across runs. The agent chooses its next steps based on tool feedback and intermediate results, while the growing context steadily inflates the input size of every subsequent call. The total consumption of a task is therefore hard to predict before execution and the prediction must be revised as the run unfolds. In this paper, we propose TokenCast, which learns a composable cost representation for each execution segment, recording its own consumption and the context growth it introduces. Composing adjacent segments yields a cumulative estimate that captures the extra input cost incurred when context from earlier segments is re-read by every later call. As execution unfolds, newly observed evidence refreshes the forecast, requiring no additional LLM calls and incurring a mean cumulative prediction time of 32.8 ms per run on SWE-bench Verified. Across 4 task suites and 6 agent models, TokenCast's mean absolute error reduction against the strongest comparator averages 14.5% over 96 evaluated combinations. In offline budget-control replay, TokenCast uses 21.3% fewer tokens on average than a fixed-budget policy at matched trace completion. The code is available at https://github.com/DEFENSE-SEU/TokenCast."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c7253619ea073aee9ba5776005e651f06fae4cf4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.34829", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34829", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34829", "CorpusId": 292435021}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c7253619ea073aee9ba5776005e651f06fae4cf4", "title": "From Weak Task Specifications to Scientific Extraction Agents: Optimizing Task Construction", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34829, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505471474", "name": "Zi-Xiao Dong"}, {"authorId": "2423982949", "name": "Wei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2332275302", "name": "Zi-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505391891", "name": "Chen-Shu Li"}, {"authorId": "2492134051", "name": "Long-Zhang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2313930198", "name": "Tao Tan"}, {"authorId": "2313467296", "name": "Hong Xie"}], "abstract": "Most methods that optimize LLM prompts and agent workflows assume that task-specific output schemas, extraction instructions, and evaluation criteria are predefined. For scientific extraction agents, however, a short task goal may not fully determine these components, while specifying them manually is costly. We study the upstream problem of constructing the task-specific configuration from a weak specification containing only a short goal and unannotated reference documents. Rather than treating automatic construction as a fixed preprocessing step, our framework constructs a task-specific schema, extraction instructions, and base training rubrics, then keeps schema construction and extraction instructions editable during optimization. Failure-focused updates concentrate textual-gradient feedback on lower-scoring documents, while training-time evaluation criteria adapt to recurring failures. On a heterogeneous-catalysis literature corpus, automatic construction remains improvable, and optimizing both schema construction and extraction instructions performs best across all four judge-rubric settings, with ablations and blinded human evaluation supporting the proposed formulation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "95ecf310b84ddc5deb72d7c376132fd2397efd25", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34179", "ArXiv": "2609.34179", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34179", "CorpusId": 292437023}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/95ecf310b84ddc5deb72d7c376132fd2397efd25", "title": "RAGWarrant: Evidence-Preserving Governance for RAG Policy Promotion Under Quality, Cost, Latency, and Risk Constraints", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34179, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505381941", "name": "R. Krueger"}, {"authorId": "2326571442", "name": "L. Krause"}, {"authorId": "2505383377", "name": "Zach Pocquette"}], "abstract": "Retrieval-augmented generation systems are extensively instrumented with metrics, benchmarks, traces, and automated judges, but these tools do not decide whether a proposed policy change is safe to release. We present RAGWarrant, an open-source promotion-control framework that treats deployment as a constrained evidence decision rather than a leaderboard choice. RAGWarrant normalizes evaluator outputs and operational telemetry, applies predeclared quality and hard-risk gates, assigns evidence-class claim ceilings, preserves negative outcomes, and emits auditable PROMOTE, BLOCK, REJECT, or INCONCLUSIVE decisions. We evaluate the framework across T2-RAGBench, MultiHop-RAG, CRAG, HotpotQA, synthetic reproduction, and bounded local generative experiments. On HotpotQA, operational savings were blocked because answer quality fell beyond the declared margin. A bounded CRAG study selected a lower-cost quality-tied policy, but related generative gains were unstable and a held-out guardrail failed closed. We claim an auditable promotion-control abstraction, not optimizer superiority, human validation, or production readiness. The tagged artifact reproduces from a fresh clone, runs as a hardened Docker job, accepts external evaluator exports, and verifies artifact integrity."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "018baad1bd6d41d8ccb19e6d2ff77c00313d3cf9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35290", "ArXiv": "2609.35290", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35290", "CorpusId": 292435937}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/018baad1bd6d41d8ccb19e6d2ff77c00313d3cf9", "title": "EvoIn: Bridging Evolution and Internalization for Agent Fine-Tuning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35290, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2042683163", "name": "Shi-Han Dou"}, {"authorId": "2362570366", "name": "Shao-Hua Liu"}, {"authorId": "2446992878", "name": "Zhong-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2399060433", "name": "Jia-Hang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2211905760", "name": "Shichun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2188630983", "name": "Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "4376097", "name": "Jiajie Jin"}, {"authorId": "2316562231", "name": "Guan-Ting Dong"}, {"authorId": "2352016379", "name": "Tao Gui"}, {"authorId": "2374299112", "name": "Qi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2496228129", "name": "Xuan-Jing Huang"}], "abstract": "Recent work has explored improving agents by jointly evolving their harnesses and models, but often takes a''potpourri''approach that bundles together new tools, new decision-making procedures, and model adaptation to the evolved harness under a single notion of agent improvement. In this paper, we instead investigate how agents can improve their decision-making procedures. In particular, we propose EvoIn, an agent fine-tuning framework that bridges evolution and internalization. EvoIn first analyzes agent execution traces to evolve and validate new decision-making procedures by temporarily instantiating them in the harness. The validated procedures guide the agent to generate improved reasoning traces. These traces are then rewritten into self-contained reasoning traces, removing explicit references to harness instructions while expressing the induced decision logic as the model's own reasoning. Finally, EvoIn fine-tunes the model on the rewritten traces, internalizing these procedures so that the improved decision-making persists without the evolved harness at inference time. We evaluate EvoIn on diverse benchmarks and find that it consistently enables agents to learn stronger decision-making procedures, raising the pass rate by 10.9 points in-domain and by 9.2 points out-of-domain. Results further show that the internalized decision procedures generalize to unseen tasks. Case studies show that agents can learn to decide how to solve a task before solving it, for example by checking a document's length to choose between reading it in full and searching it. EvoIn is also broadly applicable, showing consistent improvements on another model family."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d80791eac1f0885ae2bebb58a29cd7fd9f37ba26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.34126", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-34126", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.34126", "CorpusId": 292436517}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d80791eac1f0885ae2bebb58a29cd7fd9f37ba26", "title": "JET: Judge-Guided Evolution at Test Time for Agent Programs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34126, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2464673177", "name": "Yao-Long Teng"}, {"authorId": "2505394581", "name": "Jia-Yi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2359452955", "name": "Bo An"}], "abstract": "An agent's executable program governs how it uses tools, processes observations, and responds to failures. Evolving this program at test time can help adaptation, but deciding which changes to retain is difficult when true rewards are unavailable. Execution traces provide evidence of agent behavior, yet interpreting that evidence requires a judge that remains useful as tasks and candidate programs change. We introduce Judge-Guided Evolution at Test Time (JET), which evolves an executable judge on labeled source trajectories, then freezes and transfers it to guide target-side program evolution. The judge supplies scores and diagnostic feedback without target evaluator access or model-weight updates. On unseen WebShop tasks, JET achieves approximately 13% higher mean reward than fixed-rubric guidance when evolution begins from an unevolved program (cold start) and 4% higher when it begins from one already optimized on source tasks (warm start), with a 36% relative improvement in cold-start exact success. An exact-judge control on PushT, where the judge reconstructs the scoring rule from observations, shows that without judge error, program search becomes the bottleneck. Analyses identify useful reward-prediction logic in the evolved code and show that better final selection alone cannot explain the gains. These results support executable judge transfer for program adaptation under evaluator-preserving task shifts."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "33a91d659500aaf6205b2172e1fe127b2de806a2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.36289", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-36289", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.36289", "CorpusId": 292440934}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/33a91d659500aaf6205b2172e1fe127b2de806a2", "title": "How Much Prompt Is Enough? A Blackbox Minimization of Few-Shots in LLMs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36289, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2309176564", "name": "Ali Alfageeh"}, {"authorId": "1802682", "name": "Rahul Gopinath"}, {"authorId": "1962253", "name": "Mohammad Amin Alipour"}], "abstract": "Prompts are the primary mechanism for directing the behavior of large language models (LLMs). Yet the internal structure and causal hierarchy of prompts remain poorly understood: which parts are causally necessary and which are redundant is an open question. This opacity can have severe consequences. Subtle prompt variations can silently shift model outputs in critical software systems, and engineers lack techniques to reason about prompt reliability. We present \\framework, a blackbox prompt-minimization framework that reduces few-shot prompts to their necessary minimal subset. We use a case study to apply \\framework to a few-shot learning system and demonstrate the insights that this framework can provide. Our experiments show that few-shot exemplars can be reduced by a mean of 65.3\\%~$\\pm$~15.8\\% in character count while fully preserving propositional output fidelity. The models preferentially retain logical identifiers and constraint declarations while discarding natural language prose and cross-prompt relational annotations. Our analysis also shows that some models are universal encoders, able to produce highly legible yet minimized prompts, while others are universal decoders, able to interpret minimized prompts from most other models. By identifying which components are indispensable, \\framework provides a principled basis for prompt compression and structural analysis of few-shot exemplars."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b82bfb1facbac831c8121957d881b4445b9a475c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.33822", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-33822", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.33822", "CorpusId": 292434485}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b82bfb1facbac831c8121957d881b4445b9a475c", "title": "Vestrum: Improving Agent Harnesses by Adapting Their Verification, Structure and Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33822, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "73675649", "name": "Jayant Parashar"}, {"authorId": "2351953005", "name": "Eugene F. Douglass"}, {"authorId": "2298271075", "name": "William C. Bastian"}, {"authorId": "3422895", "name": "Suchendra Bhandarkar"}], "abstract": "An agent harness controls how a language model accesses information, uses tools, preserves memory, and checks its work. Improving this software is costly when each evaluation requires a long interaction with an environment. We introduce Vestrum, a framework that turns failures in execution traces into scoped harness changes without training the task model. Its organizing overhypothesis is that tasks of a shared kind may exhibit recurring failures whose remedies transfer within that kind. Vestrum expresses failures as recognizable classes, proposes changes across verification, retrieval, decomposition, and knowledge synthesis, and screens their scope before evaluating them as a bundle. A persistent lessons file informs subsequent proposals. Across five settings and two baseline harnesses, the frozen harnesses improve held-out performance: UltraHorizon rises from 47.6 to 59.8 over GAM, Terminal-Bench 4 Hard from 63.7% to 70.3% of checks passed over Claude Code on eight held-out tasks at 1.03x test cost, and cell-type annotation agreement from 67.5% to 77.8% on held-out sections of one slide, alongside gains on LoCoMo and AMA-Bench. Across our searches, verification grounded in evidence helped both intermediate steps and final answers, at lower cost at intermediate steps, while critics asked to rebuild finished answers broke more than they repaired. On the three memory benchmarks, Vestrum also scores above the evaluated GEPA configurations in every paired evaluation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a39b6cd8d69c23adbf0ed15ddd0388e9f768869e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32886", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32886", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32886", "CorpusId": 292434933}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a39b6cd8d69c23adbf0ed15ddd0388e9f768869e", "title": "StraTune: Adaptive Selection of Revision Operators for Self-Evolving LLM Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32886, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308072132", "name": "Ze-Ping Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505388831", "name": "Yan Li"}, {"authorId": "2135307417", "name": "Ni Lao"}, {"authorId": "2476384225", "name": "G. Wolff"}, {"authorId": "2254520205", "name": "Geng-Chen Mai"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) can learn reusable textual skills from execution feedback without updating their parameters, but effectively deciding how to revise these skills remains a key challenge. Existing methods typically rely on a fixed revision operator, a search strategy and the revision forms applied under it. However, we observe that no single revision operator consistently performs best across tasks, and repeatedly applying an unsuitable operator can limit further improvement. We propose StraTune (strategy-guided skill tuning), which lets a frozen optimizer LLM choose the revision operator at every round from the optimization state, which is defined as the current execution feedback together with the recorded outcomes of earlier strategies and forms. Candidate skills from every revision operator pass one candidate evaluation, which screens for gains and regressions on a small sample set and validates them on a larger one, and every outcome is written back to the optimization state for later choices. Across four benchmarks and two LLM settings, StraTune outperforms all five baselines in most settings. Ablations attribute the gains to the adaptive choice of the revision operator, since fixed, random, scheduled, and bandit strategy choices all score lower, and skills learned with a small target LLM also improve a stronger one. Code and learned skills are available at https://github.com/seai-lab/StraTune."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8584c011f7433215a764901c6d2342701657f04", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32452", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32452", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32452", "CorpusId": 292436806}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8584c011f7433215a764901c6d2342701657f04", "title": "What Should the Reflector See? An Empirical Study of Evidence in Reflective Prompt Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32452, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2325110888", "name": "Xiaofan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2140175677", "name": "Lu Cheng"}], "abstract": "Reflective prompt optimization revises instructions using examples of a model's behavior, but which evidence the reflector should receive remains unclear. We study evidence composition, visibility of examples, candidate selection and domain-knowledge policy within a single-parent Pareto-guided search. Using Qwen3.5-9B as both task model and reflector, we evaluate nine reflection strategies on five datasets. From these experiments, we find three distinct patterns. For performance improvement, Failures-only produces the largest mean test gain (+8.0 percentage points), while Balanced-mix and No-examples+Val share the best mean performance rank. For reflection effectiveness, No-examples achieves the best rank for improving sampled parents, yet yields only a 1.4-point mean test gain: local reflection success does not necessarily produce a stronger final prompt. For overfitting assessment, Failures-only and Balanced-mix share the lowest mean calibration-gap rank, while larger gaps on GPQA and IFBench show that calibration gains can overstate held-out improvement. This gap is a descriptive indicator, not a direct measure of overfitting. Together, these results show why reflection strategies should be assessed separately on final performance, parent improvement and calibration-to-test transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ee08c86ca9f20c0e049003ff8045ce34a796d75d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32492", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32492", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32492", "CorpusId": 292435833}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ee08c86ca9f20c0e049003ff8045ce34a796d75d", "title": "Beyond Prompt or Skill? Attribution-Guided Optimization of Modular LLM Programs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32492, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2428543236", "name": "Hao-Ran Shou"}, {"authorId": "2383913956", "name": "Hao-Yue Liu"}, {"authorId": "2164919080", "name": "Yun-Chen Huo"}, {"authorId": "2333894153", "name": "Kun Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2376127536", "name": "Xiao-Ying Tang"}], "abstract": "Large language models can solve increasingly diverse reasoning tasks, yet their performance remains highly sensitive to task prompts, intermediate instructions, and the way reusable problem-solving knowledge is incorporated. Existing optimization methods usually focus on only one part of this design space: they either optimize a monolithic prompt, or separately induce and refine skills from model traces. As a result, they lack a principled mechanism for deciding which component should be updated when failures occur, and they rarely optimize prompts, skills, and skill-use policies in a unified framework. We propose SPARO (Skill, Prompt, And Routing Optimization), a framework that jointly optimizes task instructions, reusable skill blocks, and routing rules. It performs controlled counterfactual evaluations, converts examples'effects into a probabilistic responsibility distribution over prompt, skill, and routing components, samples one component from that distribution, and applies the corresponding targeted mutation. This design moves language-program optimization beyond global prompt rewriting toward structured, reusable, and selectively activated task knowledge. Across five benchmarks and five worker models, SPARO consistently outperforms both prompt-centered and skill-centered optimization baselines. These results suggest that effective language-program optimization depends not only on discovering useful task knowledge, but also on deciding where that knowledge should be stored and when it should be activated."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d182e913fe9d2e7ddeb29f6f330fdc76d92e0f3f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32862", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32862", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32862", "CorpusId": 292435801}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d182e913fe9d2e7ddeb29f6f330fdc76d92e0f3f", "title": "RoboFoundry: System-as-Policy Evolution for Self-Learning Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32862, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2284998910", "name": "Jing-Song Liang"}, {"authorId": "2273992414", "name": "Shu-Hao Liao"}, {"authorId": "2347641227", "name": "Shi-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2205259128", "name": "Di-Yuan Hou"}, {"authorId": "2212029463", "name": "Yu-Xin Cai"}, {"authorId": "49077407", "name": "Xin-Jian Deng"}, {"authorId": "2257636330", "name": "Cheng-Yang He"}, {"authorId": "49015886", "name": "Wen-Hui Huang"}, {"authorId": "151506836", "name": "Run-Jia Tan"}, {"authorId": "2315060186", "name": "Zhi-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2476056783", "name": "Lan Yu"}, {"authorId": "2476378878", "name": "Xue-Song Tian"}, {"authorId": "2292917033", "name": "G. Sartoretti"}, {"authorId": "2504266261", "name": "Ji Luo"}, {"authorId": "2348606790", "name": "Yao Mu"}, {"authorId": "2344827849", "name": "Wen-Jun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2505375440", "name": "Wan-Hua Li"}, {"authorId": "2314963114", "name": "Chen Lv"}], "abstract": "A foundation model should not act in isolation as an embodied agent. Yet, existing methods often optimize individual components of the agent stack, such as memory, context, skills, or action interfaces, rather than treating the supporting system itself as a unified policy. Moreover, interaction alone does not yield self-improvement unless execution experience is converted into persistent, validated system changes. We therefore propose RoboFoundry, the first embodied agentic framework that formulates this process as Self-Evolving System-as-Policy. RoboFoundry diagnoses capability gaps in decision-making and memory management, converts execution traces into validated task-specific system updates, and promotes recurring improvements to the general system. Evolution operates over two complementary surfaces: a context system that manages active internal context and persistent file-system memory, and a hierarchical skill system that organizes atomic skills, reusable compositions, and failure-conditioned recovery. A shared semantic interface separates embodiment-invariant decisions from embodiment-specific execution, allowing evolved system capabilities to transfer across heterogeneous robots. On EmbodiedBench, RoboFoundry achieves state-of-the-art performance, notably improving GPT-5.5 by 27.8%. It also brings Qwen3.7-Plus to near parity with GPT-5.5 (70.3% vs. 72.7%), showing consistent gains from system-as-policy evolution across foundation models. For long-horizon memory, RoboFoundry outperforms all baselines on RoboMemArena by at least 39.0%, even against methods assisted by external foundation models. On LIBERO-PRO, it further outperforms Cap-Agent0 by 243.8%-679.7% across all perturbation types. In real-world deployments, RoboFoundry demonstrates zero-shot transfer and online evolution across robots and tasks, highlighting its potential for fully autonomous embodied agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "09df97386f7567ac2e63a8c314fbeab5beb0d3af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32498", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32498", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32498", "CorpusId": 292436862}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/09df97386f7567ac2e63a8c314fbeab5beb0d3af", "title": "DAAF: From Failure Localization to Editable System Assets in LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32498, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2383504094", "name": "Xiao-Yang Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2353428884", "name": "Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2502822098", "name": "Yu-Bin Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2134027736", "name": "Xinyi Mou"}, {"authorId": "2336921256", "name": "Zhuo-Meng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2117833477", "name": "Wenjin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2505377897", "name": "Han-Ying Jiao"}, {"authorId": "2504358350", "name": "Di Wu"}, {"authorId": "2504378833", "name": "Ming-Ye Xu"}, {"authorId": "2054618348", "name": "Yi Bin"}, {"authorId": "2383173078", "name": "Ke Feng"}, {"authorId": "2309668333", "name": "Zi-Xun Sun"}], "abstract": "Deployed LLM agents increasingly rely on persistent, versioned system assets such as routing rules, knowledge segments, prompt instructions, and reusable skills. Failure-localization methods can identify where an error manifests in an agent or execution trace, but repair requires a different decision: which editable system asset should be changed, and is that change expected to improve the task outcome? We study this gap through component-attribute failure attribution, where diagnosis targets versioned, addressable items rather than execution locations. We propose the Detection-Aware Attribution Framework (DAAF), which learns the effects of valid attribute replacements and amortizes this intervention evidence into deployment-time diagnosis. DAAF combines sparse and noisy failure signals to decide whether intervention is warranted, learns component-type-conditioned replacement effects from controlled replays evaluated by executable task outcomes, and shares supervision across requests with compatible intervention responses. At diagnosis time, DAAF uses only the observed execution, registered candidates, and available failure signals; it requires neither counterfactual replay nor task reward and returns no_change, a repair target, or an unresolved decision when evidence is insufficient. On held-out tau^2-bench Telecom tasks, DAAF achieves 80.72% attribute Hit@1, recovers 62.65% of failed executions while limiting clean-task regression to 3.23%, and reaches 71.93% overall task success. These results show that intervention-grounded attribute attribution can connect failure localization to executable system repair."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3be3409bdca38854c517c984a5629c4e102737cf", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-30967", "ArXiv": "2609.30967", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.30967", "CorpusId": 292360414}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3be3409bdca38854c517c984a5629c4e102737cf", "title": "MoMHa: Multi-Objective Optimization of LLM Harnesses over Accuracy, Safety, and Tokens", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30967, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "1930362", "name": "Subhojyoti Mukherjee"}, {"authorId": "2343233088", "name": "Mahmud Tanjim"}], "abstract": "Most work on improving large language models treats accuracy as the sole objective. We argue that the harness, the Python code surrounding the model that constructs prompts, routes calls, and parses outputs, is a first-class design surface whose quality is inherently multi-objective: an accurate harness that refuses no unsafe request, or that consumes an order of magnitude more tokens, is not a good harness. We present Meta-Harness, a system that casts harness design as search over three per-domain objectives (accuracy, behavioural safety, and token cost) solved by an agentic proposer (Claude Code) with full filesystem access to prior harness source, execution traces, and scoring artifacts. Our central finding is that a singlephase joint-reward proposer (MoMHa) outperforms every alternative, including a two-phase\"accuracy then tokens\"ablation, scalar-only feedback, and an accuracy-only baseline. We evaluate on seventeen domains: seven synthetic capability suites, seven real-world public benchmarks (HumanEval, MBPP, Spider, FEVER, MMLU-Pro, LawBench, NuminaMath), and three U-SafeBench-derived user-specific safety domains, using a 12-model fleet spanning four families. On the synthetic track MoMHa achieves a joint mean of 0.482 versus 0.198-0.422 for ten baselines, winning $7 / 10$ per-domain columns; on the real-world track it scores 0.461 versus 0.377 for the strongest baseline (DSPy), winning 5/7 columns, demonstrating that harness strategies transfer to unseen benchmarks without retraining on 8 of 12 target models. MoMHa attains the highest measured behavioral safety composite (U-SafeBench, 0.781) and uses 95 fewer tokens per example than the two-phase alternative. We will release all harness code, evaluation infrastructure, and crossmodel logs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "021d2284d1ce00e175e07c59258ee28fe2a6c6e6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.31587", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-31587", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.31587", "CorpusId": 292363637}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/021d2284d1ce00e175e07c59258ee28fe2a6c6e6", "title": "Compact Documentation for Coding Agents: A Benchmark, an Optimizer, and Why It Does Not Transfer", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31587, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2450186129", "name": "Molood Arman"}, {"authorId": "2322981055", "name": "Igor Molybog"}], "abstract": "We investigate whether natural-language documentation helps coding agents resolve software issues, and we build the tools to construct and evaluate it. We introduce a roundtrip benchmark that scores code descriptions by whether code regenerated from them passes the original tests, and show that completeness, not length, drives a description's fidelity. Using the benchmark as an optimization signal, we discover a description-writing prompt that reaches full fidelity and generalizes to unseen files. We then test the hypothesis that motivated the work: that better documentation helps an agent resolve real repository issues. Across two model families and ten repositories, and against a positive control confirming that our evaluation can detect a genuine improvement, we find that it does not. When the source is present, neither static compact documentation nor retrieved context beats the issue alone. We report this negative result together with the benchmark and the optimizer, and we characterize the boundary at which documentation helps."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f1af852a788cb953d6b64e238b183885b7b5c817", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.32092", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-32092", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.32092", "CorpusId": 292409053}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f1af852a788cb953d6b64e238b183885b7b5c817", "title": "On Evaluating and Improving Conversational Agents in Production", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32092, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2321591606", "name": "Kasra Hosseini"}, {"authorId": "2211998291", "name": "Wen-Sen Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2470090365", "name": "Marco-Andrea Buchmann"}, {"authorId": "2505056676", "name": "Emir Mulabegovic"}, {"authorId": "2316478440", "name": "Wei-Wei Cheng"}], "abstract": "We present a framework for evaluating and improving a large-scale, multi-agent shopping assistant in production, and report lessons from its use. Offline evaluation of such a system faces three obstacles. (i) A logged conversation cannot be replayed against a modified system, because a different response changes every turn that follows. (ii) The unchanged system itself varies from run to run. Its LLM components are stochastic, and in product search the available products, their prices, and the customer's personalization signals change. (iii) Aggregate quality scores combine distinct behaviors, so they show that quality has changed but not which behavior caused the change. Our framework addresses each obstacle in turn. For a reported behavior, an Evaluation Harness generates targeted assertions and a fixed cohort of customer scenarios. It then reproduces the behavior in a local instance of the assistant through grounded user simulation. Instead of replaying the log, the simulator writes new customer turns conditioned on the recorded messages and context. Repeated runs of the unchanged system form a stored baseline. An Improvement Orchestrator turns the assertion results into hypotheses, implements each as an isolated modification, and compares it with the baseline using paired percentile bootstrap intervals over scenario-level differences. When an investigation ends, the harness may propose revisions to future evaluations, subject to human approval and without altering past decisions. We report production investigations with this framework. Assertion profiles showed which positions of a product carousel a failure affected, and repeated runs distinguished a real improvement from run-to-run fluctuation. Audits of the evaluation itself found a judge that lacked the evidence it needed and a model setting that was configured but not applied."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9d764f959560e32f987cba0f8532a82d60d0410c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.31989", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-31989", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.31989", "CorpusId": 292408989}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9d764f959560e32f987cba0f8532a82d60d0410c", "title": "Communication between Frozen Large Language Models via Prompt Optimization in a Referential Game", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31989, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2484352147", "name": "V. Anand"}, {"authorId": "2386804", "name": "Muthu Kumar Chandrasekaran"}, {"authorId": "145057727", "name": "Shiva Chaitanya"}], "abstract": "We study communication between two frozen large language models from different providers, with different tokenizers, accessed through their API endpoints. The two play a referential game: one sees an object and describes it in a short fixed-length message over a small alphabet; the other must pick that object out of a candidate set. Neither model's weights are updated. Each agent's prompt is rewritten by an isolated prompt optimizer whose reflection model reads that agent's scored interactions. In the positional setting, optimized prompts carry a shared code that generalizes to held-out objects above a measured no-codebook baseline, including when the memory window is removed. In a second setting, independent per-letter blocks no longer fit within the message, although a whole-object place value code does. The base system fails to establish reliable communication: the sender struggles to retain an injective rule, and the receiver has too few confirmed examples in view. A sender collision penalty, retention of successful interactions, and sequential optimization enable successful place value communication in some runs. Outcomes vary across runs and reflection models. In successful runs, the protocol is written into the optimized prompts, where it can be read and audited directly."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "856312050b8b9686dcddd4d703149309d5714a94", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.35855", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-35855", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.35855", "CorpusId": 292442070}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/856312050b8b9686dcddd4d703149309d5714a94", "title": "Mara Chain: Rethinking Failure as a Stepping Stone for AI System Auto-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35855, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505455537", "name": "Yubin Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2268097533", "name": "Fu Li"}, {"authorId": "2505450697", "name": "Jia-Wei Fei"}, {"authorId": "2366209057", "name": "Yang Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2505455055", "name": "Wei-Xing Mei"}, {"authorId": "2504189761", "name": "Yi-Nan Wu"}], "abstract": "Optimizing deployed AI systems increasingly amounts to editing prompts, skills, harnesses, and code rather than model weights. Existing approaches commonly optimize these artifacts through propose-evaluate-select procedures, where candidate configurations are evaluated and only those meeting an acceptance criterion are selected. Yet our analysis shows that discarded candidates often contain information critical for subsequent optimization. Discarding them causes later proposals to revisit the same failure modes. We introduce Mara Chain, a refinement procedure that turns rejected candidates into stepping stones. Rather than discarding a rejected candidate, Mara Chain retains and iteratively refines it using evidence accumulated across preceding attempts. The procedure limits each refinement chain to a fixed depth and applies Pareto-filtered Top-N selection to bound the candidate pool. Across AppWorld skill optimization, TerminalBench 2.1 harness optimization, and MuSiQue retrieval-pipeline optimization, Mara Chain delivers greater task-performance gains with fewer rollouts. It outperforms GEPA, ACE, and SkillOpt-Lite by up to 20.5% in relative performance on AppWorld, reaching the target score with 65.5% fewer rollouts than GEPA. It improves the pass rate by 20.2 and 22.5 percentage points over AHE and Meta-Harness on TerminalBench 2.1, respectively, and improves MuSiQue test nDCG@10 and Recall@10 by 0.104 and 0.131 over a hand-written retrieval pipeline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "493bcca6d8f7d9c914a74c7e4ee1163a81487577", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-28908", "ArXiv": "2609.28908", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.28908", "CorpusId": 292282205}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/493bcca6d8f7d9c914a74c7e4ee1163a81487577", "title": "Automatic Harness Evolution for Hardware Design Verification: Can LLMs Consolidate Gains Across Discovered Harnesses?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28908, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2502988561", "name": "Kidus Seyoum"}, {"authorId": "2502988523", "name": "Ajay Mittur"}], "abstract": "Agent behavior depends on the harness surrounding a language model, but it remains unclear whether language models can reliably improve such harnesses for hardware-design tasks. We study automatic harness evolution around a fixed subject model on 12 proprietary design-verification root-cause localization tasks. Across five trials per task, automatically evolved harnesses increased completed attempts by 71-76% and any-hit task coverage by 80-100%, while total correct attempts improved by only 18-24%. The strongest success reproducible at least twice result improved by one task, and later candidates exchanged gains across tasks rather than preserving them. An auxiliary candidate improved on a four-task validation set excluded from search but tied its baseline on a subsequent 12-task replay containing both search and validation tasks, so the selected gain did not persist across the full pool. Across the tested lineage, useful search, evidence, and finalization behaviors appeared in different candidates but did not consistently consolidate into a single harness that dominated across tasks and metrics. In a separate CVDP cross-benchmark case study, an automatically evolved defined-width repair harness produced 35.6% more functional passes than its 142-task reference baseline; the final functional verifier scored completed outputs but was not shown to the subject agent during repair. These results support archive-aware selection when evolution yields complementary specializations without consistent consolidation."}}]}
